window.EF_CRUX_DOC_PAGES=["ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.1 Physical Quantities & Units\nPHYSICAL QUANTITIES & UNITS — MEMORY TRICK SHEET\nGeneral Science · Physics · Chapter 1 · Units + SI + Practical Units + Prefixes + Dimensions\nYe full notes nahi hai — source chapter ke scalar-vector pairs, unit systems, SI units, practical units, derived-unit confusions,\nprefixes aur dimensional formulae ko exam-recall tricks mein compress kiya gaya hai.\n1. Physical Quantity — Measurable + Number\n• Physical quantity = directly ya indirectly measurable property\n• Fixed numerical value ke saath express ki jaati hai\n• Examples: Mass, Length, Force\nTRICK - “Measure + Number = Physical Quantity”\nNaam ya concept tab physical quantity banta hai jab measurement ke context mein express ho.\n2. Scalar vs Vector — M vs M+D\nScalar = Magnitude Only\nVector = Magnitude + Direction\nDistance\nDisplacement\nSpeed\nVelocity\nMass\nForce\nWork\nAcceleration\nTime\nMomentum\nEnergy\n—\nElectric current\n—\nTemperature\n—\nPressure\n—\nTRICK - “Scalar = Sirf Size; Vector = Size + Side”\nMagnitude only = Scalar.\nMagnitude + direction = Vector.\nEXAM TRAP - “D-S-M / D-V-F”\nDistance-Speed-Mass = Scalar.\nDisplacement-Velocity-Force = Vector.\n3. Fundamental - Derived - Supplementary\nType\nDependency\nExamples\nFundamental\nIndependent\nLength, Mass, Time\nDerived\nFundamental quantities par dependent\nForce, Work, Pressure\nSupplementary\nSource convention: 2 quantities\nPlane angle, Solid angle\nTRICK - “F = Free, D = Dependent, S = 2 Angles”\nFundamental independent; Derived depends; Supplementary source convention mein Plane + Solid angle.\nEXAM TRAP -\nSource notes convention = 7 fundamental + 2 supplementary SI units.\n4. Unit Systems — F-C-M-S\nSystem\nIdentity\nL-M-T\nSource Key Fact\nFPS\nBritish\nFoot-Pound-Second\n1 Pound = 453 gm; source says\nIndia 1826","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.1 Physical Quantities & Units\nCGS\nFrench / Metric\ncm-g-s\nSource says India\nimplementation 1956\nMKS\n—\nm-kg-s\nDeveloped in France\nSI\nInternational\nm-kg-s\nSource links 1960 Paris\nconference; 7+2\nTRICK - “FPS = Foot Pound Second; CGS = Centi Gram Second; MKS = Meter Kilo Second”\nSystem ka naam hi L-M-T base set reveal karta hai.\nTRICK - “SI = 1960 Paris + 7+2”\nSource chapter ka historical recall pair.\n5. Seven Fundamental SI Units — LMT-TELA\nQuantity\nUnit\nSymbol\nLength\nMeter\nm\nMass\nKilogram\nkg\nTime\nSecond\ns\nTemperature\nKelvin\nK\nElectric Current\nAmpere\nA\nLuminous Intensity\nCandela\ncd\nAmount of Substance\nMole\nmol\nTRICK - “LMT-TELA”\nLength - Mass - Time - Temperature - Electric current - Luminous intensity - Amount of substance.\nTRICK - “m kg s / K A cd mol”\nUnit-symbol chain ko 3 + 4 ke block mein bolo.\nSupplementary Quantity\nUnit\nSymbol\nPlane Angle\nRadian\nrad\nSolid Angle\nSteradian\nsr\n6. Astronomy Distance Trio — PC > LY > AU\nUnit\nValue\nUse\nParsec\n3.26 light years = 3.08 x 10^16 m\nAstronomical distance\nLight Year\n9.46 x 10^15 m\nLight travels in one year\nAU\n1.496 x 10^11 m\nEarth-Sun distance\nTRICK - “P-L-A = Parsec > Light Year > AU”\nDistance order: PC > LY > AU.\nEXAM TRAP - “Parsec = 3.26 LIGHT YEARS, not AU”\nDirect source trap.\n7. Tiny / Atomic / FPS Units\nUnit\nValue / Equivalent\nHook\nAngstrom\n10^-10 m\nAtomic distance\namu\n1.66 x 10^-27 kg\nAtomic mass\nSlug\n14.59 kg\nFPS mass\nSvedberg\n10^-13 s\nSedimentation rate","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.1 Physical Quantities & Units\nTRICK - “A-10, amu-27, Svedberg-13”\nAngstrom = 10^-10 m.\namu = 1.66 x 10^-27 kg.\nSvedberg = 10^-13 s.\n8. Special Practical Units — D-C-B-C\nUnit / Limit\nUsed For / Value\nDobson\nOzone layer thickness\nCusec\nFlow of water\nBTU\nHeat\nBarrel\n158.98 litres; oil volume\nChandrasekhar Limit\n1.4 solar masses; white dwarf maximum mass\nTRICK - “Dobson Ozone - Cusec Water - BTU Heat - Barrel Oil”\nSpecial units ko use ke saath pair karo.\nEXAM TRAP - “Chandra = 1.4 Sun”\nChandrasekhar limit = 1.4 solar masses, source notes ke mutabik.\n9. Derived Units — J-N-W-P\nQuantity\nUnit\nSymbol\nWork / Energy\nJoule\nJ\nForce\nNewton\nN\nPower\nWatt\nW\nPressure\nPascal\nPa\nPotential Difference\nVolt\nV\nResistance\nOhm\nOmega\nCapacitance\nFarad\nF\nMagnetic Flux\nWeber\nWb\nFrequency\nHertz\nHz\nCharge\nCoulomb\nC\nTRICK - “Work J, Force N, Power W, Pressure Pa”\nJ-N-W-P = four core mechanical derived units.\nTRICK - “V-O-F-Wb-Hz-C”\nVolt - Ohm - Farad - Weber - Hertz - Coulomb.\nEXAM TRAP - “Weber = Flux; Watt = Power”\nWb aur W ka symbol confusion avoid karo.\n10. Derived Unit Specials — Impulse = Momentum\nQuantity\nUnit\nMomentum\nkg m/s\nImpulse\nN s\nSurface Tension\nN/m\nAngular Velocity\nrad/s\nIlluminance\nLux\nLuminous Flux\nLumen","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.1 Physical Quantities & Units\nTRICK - “Impulse = Force x Time = Ns = Momentum pattern”\nImpulse aur momentum ka dimensional formula same = [MLT^-1].\n11. Prefix Ladder — Exa to Atto\nPower\nPrefix\nSymbol\nPower\nPrefix\nSymbol\n10^18\nExa\nE\n10^-1\nDeci\nd\n10^15\nPeta\nP\n10^-2\nCenti\nc\n10^12\nTera\nT\n10^-3\nMilli\nm\n10^9\nGiga\nG\n10^-6\nMicro\nmu\n10^6\nMega\nM\n10^-9\nNano\nn\n10^3\nKilo\nk\n10^-12\nPico\np\n10^2\nHecto\nh\n10^-15\nFemto\nf\n10^1\nDeca\nda\n10^-18\nAtto\na\nTRICK - “Large: E-P-T-G-M-k-h-da”\n18-15-12-9-6-3-2-1.\nTRICK - “Small: d-c-m-mu-n-p-f-a”\n-1,-2,-3,-6,-9,-12,-15,-18.\nTRICK - “Mega +6 / Micro -6; Giga +9 / Nano -9; Tera +12 / Pico -12”\nOpposite-sign mirror pairs.\nEXAM TRAP - “Kilo = k, Kelvin = K”\nCase-sensitive symbol trap.\n12. Dimensions — Core Ladder\nQuantity\nDimensional Formula\nDensity\n[ML^-3]\nVelocity / Speed\n[LT^-1]\nAcceleration\n[LT^-2]\nMomentum / Impulse\n[MLT^-1]\nForce\n[MLT^-2]\nPressure / Stress / Modulus\n[ML^-1T^-2]\nWork / Energy\n[ML^2T^-2]\nPower\n[ML^2T^-3]\nSurface Tension\n[MT^-2]\nFrequency\n[T^-1]\nTRICK - “v - a - F - W - P = T exponent -1,-2,-2,-2,-3”\nVelocity LT^-1; Acceleration LT^-2; Force MLT^-2; Work ML^2T^-2; Power ML^2T^-3.\nTRICK - “Momentum = Impulse = MLT^-1”\nSame formula pair.\nEXAM TRAP - “Pressure Family = P-S-M = ML^-1T^-2”\nPressure + Stress + Modulus of Elasticity.\n13. Unique Dimension Patterns\nQuantity\nUnique Pattern","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.1 Physical Quantities & Units\nFrequency\n[T^-1] only\nSurface Tension\n[MT^-2] - no L\nDensity\n[ML^-3] - no T\nSpecific Heat\n[L^2T^-2K^-1]\nResistance\n[ML^2T^-3A^-2]\nCapacitance\n[M^-1L^-2T^4A^2]\nElectric Field\n[MLT^-3A^-1]\nTRICK - “Freq only T; Surface no L; Density no T”\nFast elimination ke 3 strongest unique patterns.\n14. Final Exam Traps — 10 Red Flags\n• Speed scalar vs Velocity vector\n• Distance scalar vs Displacement vector\n• Mass scalar vs Force vector\n• Source SI convention = 7 fundamental + 2 supplementary\n• 1 Parsec = 3.26 light years, not AU\n• Distance order = Parsec > Light Year > AU\n• Magnetic flux = Weber (Wb), Power = Watt (W)\n• Momentum = Impulse = [MLT^-1]\n• Pressure = Stress = Modulus = [ML^-1T^-2]\n• Kilo symbol k, Kelvin symbol K; Dobson-Ozone, Cusec-Water, BTU-Heat\nEXAM TRAP - “S-V / D-D / M-F / 7+2 / P-L-A / Wb-W / M=I / PSM / k-K / DCB”\n10 traps ko isi short chain mein revise karo.\nMASTER MEMORY CHAIN — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\nMASTER - 1-Line Revision Chain\n“Physical quantity = measurable + number\n Scalar sirf magnitude, Vector magnitude+direction; D-S-M scalar vs D-V-F vector\n\n→\n→\nFundamental Free, Derived Dependent, Supplementary source 2 angles\n systems FPS Foot-Pound-Second, CGS cm-g-s, MKS/SI m-kg-\n→\ns; SI source 1960 Paris + 7+2\n LMT-TELA = m kg s K A cd mol; Plane rad, Solid sr\n astronomy P-L-A = Parsec > Light Year > AU;\n→\n→\nParsec = 3.26 LY\n Angstrom 10^-10 m, amu 1.66x10^-27 kg, Slug14.59kg, Svedberg10^-13s\n Dobson ozone, Cusec water, BTU heat,\n→\n→\nBarrel oil, Chandra1.4Sun\n derived J-N-W-P; V-Ohm-Farad-Weber-Hertz-Coulomb; Weber flux not Watt; Impulse Ns = Momentum\n→\nkg m/s\n prefix big E-P-T-G-M-k-h-da and small d-c-m-mu-n-p-f-a; Mega/Micro +/-6, Giga/Nano +/-9, Tera/Pico +/-12; k vs K\n\n→\n→\ndimensions: Density ML^-3, v LT^-1, a LT^-2, Momentum/Impulse MLT^-1, Force MLT^-2, P-S-Modulus ML^-1T^-2, Work ML^2T^-2,\nPower ML^2T^-3, Surface MT^-2, Frequency T^-1.”\nTOP 5: Scalar/Vector pairs | LMT-TELA | Parsec=3.26 LY + P>LY>AU | Weber vs Watt | Momentum=Impulse &\nPressure=Stress=Modulus dimensions.","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.2 Motion\nMOTION — MEMORY TRICK SHEET\nGeneral Science · Physics · Chapter 2 · Motion Types + Distance/Displacement + Graphs + Projectile + Circular Motion\nYe full notes nahi hai — source chapter ke 1D/2D/3D motion, distance-displacement, speed-velocity, equations, graph rules,\nprojectile formulae aur circular-motion traps ko exam-recall tricks mein compress kiya gaya hai.\n1. Motion Types — S-R-O\nType\nMeaning\nExamples\nStraight Line\nStraight path\nRunning child; straight-road car\nRotational\nOwn axis about circular path\nFan; bicycle wheel\nOscillatory\nBack-and-forth about mean position\nPendulum; sewing-machine needle\nTRICK - “S-R-O = Straight - Rotate - Oscillate”\nStraight = line; Rotational = own axis; Oscillatory = mean position ke around back-forth.\n2. Dimension of Motion — 1 Car, 2 Projectile, 3 Mosquito\nDimension\nCoordinates\nExamples\n1D\nx only\nCar on straight road\n2D\nx + y\nProjectile, circular motion, planet around\nSun\n3D\nx + y + z\nMosquito, bird, water molecules\nTRICK - “1-Car / 2-Projectile-Circle / 3-Mosquito”\n1D straight road, 2D projectile/circular, 3D flying mosquito.\nEXAM TRAP -\nProjectile = 2D; Circular motion = 2D; Mosquito flying = 3D.\n3. Uniform vs Non-Uniform — Equal vs Unequal\nMotion\nCondition\nDistance-Time Graph\nUniform\nEqual distances in equal time intervals\nStraight line; constant slope\nNon-Uniform\nUnequal distances in equal time intervals\nCurved line\nTRICK - “Uniform = Equal-Equal = Constant Speed”\nDistance-time graph ki constant slope speed ko show karti hai.\n4. Distance vs Displacement — Path vs Shortcut\nFeature\nDistance\nDisplacement\nMeaning\nActual path length\nShortest initial-to-final path\nQuantity\nScalar\nVector\nValue\nAlways positive\nPositive, negative or zero\nFormula\nLength of path\nFinal position - Initial position\nTRICK - “Distance = Drive Path; Displacement = Direct Shortcut”\nDistance scalar; displacement vector.\nEXAM TRAP - “Go 50 + Return 50 = Distance 100, Displacement 0”\nObject move kare to distance zero nahi; displacement zero ho sakta hai.","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.2 Motion\n5. Speed vs Velocity — d/t vs x/t\nFeature\nSpeed\nVelocity\nMeaning\nDistance/time\nDisplacement/time + direction\nQuantity\nScalar\nVector\nValue\nAlways positive\nPositive, negative or zero\nAverage\nTotal distance / Total time\nTotal displacement / Total time\nTRICK - “Speed = Scalar + Distance; Velocity = Vector + Displacement”\nS-D / V-D pairing.\nEXAM TRAP -\nSpeed scalar; Velocity vector. Same SI unit m/s, nature different.\n6. Acceleration — Change in Velocity\n• Acceleration = rate of change of velocity\n• a = Delta v / Delta t = (v-u)/t\n• Average acceleration = (v2-v1)/(t2-t1)\n• Vector quantity; SI unit m/s^2\n• Can be positive, negative or zero\n• Negative acceleration = deceleration / retardation\nTRICK - “Acceleration = Velocity Change / Time”\nNegative a = deceleration/retardation.\nEXAM TRAP -\nNegative acceleration ko speed ke saath confuse mat karo; source terminology = deceleration/retardation.\n7. Motion Symbols — U-V-A-T-S\nSymbol\nMeaning\nu\nInitial velocity\nv\nFinal velocity\na\nAcceleration\nt\nTime\ns\nDisplacement\nTRICK - “U-V-A-T-S”\nInitial-Final-Acceleration-Time-Displacement.\n8. Three Equations — V-S-V²\nNo.\nEquation\nUse\n1\nv = u + at\nFinal velocity after time t\n2\ns = ut + 1/2 at^2\nDisplacement after time t\n3\nv^2 = u^2 + 2as\nVelocity-displacement relation\nTRICK - “1st v, 2nd s, 3rd v-square”\nEquation order ko variable se yaad karo: v > s > v^2.\nTRICK - “Free Fall = a\ng, s\nh”\n→\n→\nv = u+gt; h = ut+1/2 gt^2; v^2 = u^2+2gh.","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.2 Motion\n9. Graph Master Rule — Slope-Slope-Area\nGraph\nSlope Gives\nArea Rule\nDistance-Time\nSpeed\n—\nDisplacement-Time\nVelocity\n—\nVelocity-Time\nAcceleration\nArea = Displacement\nTRICK - “s-t Slope = Speed/Velocity; v-t Slope = Acceleration; v-t Area = Displacement”\nGraph questions ka master three-rule.\n10. s-t Graph Shapes — Rest / Uniform / Accelerate / Decelerate\nShape\nMotion\nHorizontal line\nRest; v=0\nStraight positive slope\nUniform motion\nUpward curve\nUniform accelerated motion\nDownward curve\nUniform decelerated motion\nEXAM TRAP - “s-t Horizontal = STOP”\nHorizontal s-t line means REST, not uniform motion.\n11. v-t Graph Shapes — Constant / +Slope / -Slope\nShape\nMotion\nHorizontal line\nUniform motion; v constant; a=0\nStraight + slope\nUniform acceleration\nStraight - slope\nUniform deceleration\nUpward curve\nNon-uniform acceleration\nTRICK - “v-t Horizontal = GO Constant”\ns-t horizontal = rest, but v-t horizontal = uniform motion.\nEXAM TRAP - “s-t H = Rest; v-t H = Uniform”\nSame horizontal line, different meaning.\n12. Projectile Formulae — T-H-R\nQuantity\nFormula\nTime of Flight T\n2u sinθ / g\nMaximum Height H\nu^2 sin^2θ / 2g\nRange R\nu^2 sin2θ / g\nTRICK - “Projectile = T-H-R”\nTime-Height-Range formula trio.\n• Path = Parabola\n• Horizontal velocity remains constant if air resistance neglected\n• Vertical velocity changes due to gravity\n• At highest point vertical velocity = 0\nTRICK - “Top Point = Vertical 0, Horizontal Continues”\nHighest point par only vertical component zero.","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.2 Motion\n13. Projectile Angles — 45 Range, 90 Height\nCondition\nSource Result\nθ = 45°\nMaximum Range\nθ = 90°\nMaximum Height\nAt maximum range\nRmax = 4 x Hmax - as stated in source notes\nTRICK - “45 = Range; 90 = Height”\n45 ko height aur 90 ko range mat karna.\nTRICK - “Projectile = Parabola”\nSource ideal condition under no-air-resistance framing.\nEXAM TRAP -\nSource notes state: 45° maximum range, 90° maximum height, and Rmax = 4 x Hmax.\n14. Circular Motion — Constant Speed, Changing Velocity\n• Uniform circular motion: speed constant, velocity variable because direction changes\n• Non-uniform circular motion: speed and velocity both variable\n• Examples: electron around nucleus; Moon around Earth\nTRICK - “Circle = Speed Same, Direction Changes, So Velocity Changes”\nUniform circular motion accelerated motion hai because velocity direction changes.\nEXAM TRAP -\nConstant speed ka matlab zero acceleration nahi in uniform circular motion.\n15. Circular Formula Pack — Theta-Omega-v-alpha-a\nQuantity\nFormula / Direction\nAngular displacement θ\nArc Δs / radius r\nAngular velocity ω\nΔθ / Δt\nLinear-angular relation\nv = ωr = 2πnr\nAngular acceleration α\nα = ω/t - source formula\nCentripetal acceleration\na = v^2/r = rω^2; towards centre\nTRICK - “θ\n ω\n v = ωr\n a_c = v²/r”\n→\n→\n→\nCircular-motion formula chain.\nEXAM TRAP - “Centripetal = Centre; Centrifugal = Outward Pseudo”\nDirection trap.","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.2 Motion\n16. Master Scalar-Vector Table\nQuantity\nNature\nUnit\nDistance\nScalar\nm\nDisplacement\nVector\nm\nSpeed\nScalar\nm/s\nVelocity\nVector\nm/s\nAcceleration\nVector\nm/s^2\nAngular velocity\nVector\nrad/s\nAngular acceleration\nVector\nrad/s^2\nTRICK - “D-S Scalar; D-V-A-ω-α Vector”\nDistance + Speed = scalar.\nDisplacement + Velocity + Acceleration + angular quantities = vector.\n17. Final Exam Traps — 8 Red Flags\n• Projectile + circular motion = 2D; straight-road car = 1D; mosquito = 3D\n• Distance scalar and always positive; displacement vector and can be zero\n• Speed scalar; velocity and acceleration vectors\n• Negative acceleration = deceleration / retardation\n• s-t slope = speed/velocity; v-t slope = acceleration; v-t area = displacement\n• Horizontal s-t = rest; horizontal v-t = uniform motion\n• Projectile: 45° maximum range; 90° maximum height; path parabola\n• Uniform circular motion = constant speed but variable velocity, so acceleration non-zero\nEXAM TRAP - “2D-3D / D-D / S-V-A / Negative-a / Slope-Area / Horizontal / 45-90 / Circle”\nChapter ke 8 major traps isi chain mein revise karo.\nMASTER MEMORY CHAIN — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\nMASTER - 1-Line Revision Chain\n“Motion S-R-O = Straight-Rotational-Oscillatory\n dimensions 1-Car, 2-Projectile/Circle, 3-Mosquito\n Uniform equal-equal constant\n→\n→\nspeed, Nonuniform unequal\n Distance path-scalar-positive vs Displacement shortcut-vector +/-/0; Speed distance/time scalar vs\n→\nVelocity displacement/time vector\n Acceleration Δv/Δt, negative = deceleration; UVATS symbols\n equations 1st v=u+at, 2nd\n→\n→\ns=ut+1/2at², 3rd v²=u²+2as; free fall a\ng,s\nh\n graphs: s-t slope speed/velocity, v-t slope acceleration, v-t area displacement; s-t\n→\n→\n→\nhorizontal REST, v-t horizontal UNIFORM\n Projectile THR = 2usinθ/g, u²sin²θ/2g, u²sin2θ/g; path parabola; top vertical v=0; 45\n→\nrange, 90 height; source Rmax=4xHmax\n Circular: constant speed but variable velocity; θ=arc/r, ω=Δθ/Δt, v=ωr=2πnr, source α=ω/t,\n→\ncentripetal a=v²/r=rω² toward centre; centrifugal outward pseudo.”\nTOP 5: Distance vs Displacement | Speed vs Velocity | s-t/v-t slope-area rules | 45° Range vs 90° Height | Uniform circular motion =\nconstant speed but non-zero acceleration.","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.3 Laws of Force & Motion\nLAWS OF FORCE & MOTION — MEMORY TRICK SHEET\nGeneral Science · Physics · Chapter 3 · Force + Newton Laws + Momentum + Friction + Circular Force + Torque + Lever\nYe full notes nahi hai — source chapter ke force-types, Newton’s laws, momentum-impulse, friction order, centripetal/centrifugal\nforce, torque-couple aur lever classes ko fast exam-recall tricks mein compress kiya gaya hai.\n1. Force — Push/Pull + Shape/Direction/Motion\n• Force object ki shape, direction ya state of motion ko change kar sakta hai\n• Daily-life form = push, pull or hit\n• SI unit = Newton (N); CGS unit = Dyne\n• 1 N = 10^5 dyne; 1 dyne = 10^-5 N\nTRICK - “Force = Shape - Side - State”\nShape, direction aur state of motion ko change karne wala external factor.\nTRICK - “N to Dyne = ×10^5”\n1 Newton = 100000 dyne.\n2. Force Types — C/N + B/U\nType\nMeaning\nExamples / Result\nContact\nPhysical contact required\nFrictional, muscular\nNon-Contact\nNo physical contact\nGravitational, magnetic\nBalanced\nResultant = 0\na = 0; rest OR constant velocity\nUnbalanced\nResultant ≠ 0\na ≠ 0; motion state changes\nTRICK - “C-N = Contact-No Contact; B-U = Balance-Unbalance”\nBalanced = net force zero.\nUnbalanced = net force non-zero.\nTRICK - “200 opposite 200 = 0; 400 opposite 200 = 200 toward larger”\nSource example se resultant-force arithmetic lock karo.\nEXAM TRAP -\nBalanced force ka matlab hamesha rest nahi; constant velocity bhi ho sakti hai.\n3. Newton’s First Law — Inertia\n• Object at rest remains at rest; moving object uniform straight-line motion continue karta hai unless external force acts\n• Also called Law of Inertia\n• Associated with Galileo’s idea of inertia\n• Inertia = change oppose karne ki tendency\n• Inertia depends on mass; greater mass = greater inertia\n• Types = inertia of rest, motion and direction\nTRICK - “1st = Inertia; Mass = Measure of Inertia”\nFirst law qualitative idea of force deta hai.\nTRICK - “R-M-D = Rest - Motion - Direction”\nInertia ke 3 types.\nEXAM TRAP -\nF = ma First Law se nahi; Second Law se milta hai.","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.3 Laws of Force & Motion\n4. Momentum + Newton’s Second Law\n• Linear momentum p = m × v\n• Momentum vector quantity hai\n• SI unit = kg·m/s\n• Newton’s Second Law: rate of change of momentum applied force ke proportional\n• For constant mass: F = ma\nTRICK - “p = mv; 2nd = dp/dt = ma”\nMomentum formula aur quantitative force law ek chain.\nEXAM TRAP - “1 Inertia - 2 F=ma - 3 Action/Reaction”\nNewton laws ka master code.\n5. Impulse — Force-Time = Momentum Change\n• Impulse I = F × Δt\n• I = Δp = m(v-u)\n• SI unit = N·s\n• Examples: hammer strike; catch lete waqt player hands backward move karta hai\nTRICK - “I = FΔt = Δp”\nImpulse = force-time product = change in momentum.\nTRICK - “10 g, 0\n10 m/s = 0.1 N·s”\n→\nSource worked example: 0.01 × 10 = 0.1 N·s.\n6. Newton’s Third Law — Action-Reaction\n• Every action has equal and opposite reaction\n• F1 = -F2\n• Equal magnitude, opposite direction\n• Action-reaction forces different bodies par act karte hain\n• Examples: rocket launch, rowing, swimming, bouncing ball, gun recoil\nTRICK - “3rd = Equal-Opposite-Different Bodies”\nDifferent bodies sabse important trap.\nEXAM TRAP -\nAction-reaction forces cancel nahi karte because they act on different bodies.\n7. Conservation of Linear Momentum\n• Net external force = 0 ho to total linear momentum constant\n• F = dp/dt = 0 > p = constant\n• m1u1 + m2u2 = m1v1 + m2v2\n• Examples: rocket propulsion, gun recoil, person jumps from boat\nTRICK - “External Force ZERO\n Total Momentum SAME”\n→\nMomentum conservation ki necessary source condition.\nTRICK - “10×20 + 4×(-20) = 14v\n v = 8.57 m/s right”\n→\nSource collision example ka final answer.","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.3 Laws of Force & Motion\nEXAM TRAP -\nRocket motion source mein momentum conservation + Newton’s Third Law dono se linked hai.\n8. Friction — Formula + Cause\n• Friction contact surfaces ke along tangentially act karta hai\n• Relative motion ko oppose karta hai\n• Cause: surface irregularities + molecular bonding at contact points\n• F = μR\nTRICK - “Friction = μ × Normal Reaction”\nF = μR; μ = coefficient of friction.\n9. Friction Types — L-S-S-R\nType\nMeaning / Condition\nStatic\nBefore motion; self-adjusting\nLimiting\nMaximum static; body about to move; Fs = μsR\nKinetic\nMoving contact surfaces\nSliding\nType of kinetic friction\nRolling\nLeast among listed friction types\nTRICK - “Limiting > Static > Sliding > Rolling”\nSource friction order.\nTRICK - “Rolling = Least\n Wheels/Ball Bearings”\n→\nFriction reduction ka reason.\nEXAM TRAP -\nLimiting friction = maximum static friction.\n10. Friction — Uses, Losses, Control\nIncrease Friction\nDecrease Friction\nGrooves in tyres\nPolish rough surfaces\nSand on snow roads\nLubricants\nRough shoe soles\nLow-friction materials\n—\nBall bearings\n—\nStreamlined shapes in fluids\nTRICK - “Grip = Grooves-Sand-Rough; Glide = Polish-Lube-Bearing-Streamline”\nIncrease vs decrease friction quick split.\n• Advantages: walking, brakes, writing on blackboard\n• Disadvantages: energy loss, lower machine efficiency, wear & tear","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.3 Laws of Force & Motion\n11. Centripetal vs Centrifugal\nFeature\nCentripetal\nCentrifugal\nDirection\nTowards centre\nAway from centre\nNature\nReal/resultant inward force\nApparent / pseudo in rotating frame\nMagnitude form\nF = mv²/r\nSame magnitude form; opposite apparent\ndirection\nExamples\nCar circular path; electron around nucleus\nCream separator; washing-machine dryer\nTRICK - “PETAL Pulls IN; FUGAL Flies OUT”\nCentripetal inward real; centrifugal outward pseudo.\nEXAM TRAP -\nCentripetal = centreward real; Centrifugal = outward apparent/pseudo.\n12. Circular Road Formulae\nSituation\nFormula\nMaximum speed on level circular road\nvmax = (μsrg)\n√\nBanking relation\ntan θ = v²/(rg)\nTRICK - “Level Road = μrg; Bank = tanθ = v²/rg”\n√\nCircular-road formula pair.\n13. Torque + Couple\n• Torque = force ka turning tendency about fixed axis\n• τ = r × F = rF sinθ\n• Vector quantity\n• SI unit = N·m\n• Couple = two equal and opposite parallel forces with separated lines of action\n• Moment of couple = Force × perpendicular separation\nTRICK - “Torque = rF sinθ; Unit N·m”\nTurning effect formula + unit.\nTRICK - “Couple = Equal-Opposite-Parallel + Gap”\nGap = perpendicular distance between force lines.\nEXAM TRAP -\nTorque unit N·m hai; source chapter mein vector quantity bhi stated hai.\n14. Lever Basics — P-F-W + Arms\nTerm\nMeaning\nEffort P\nApplied force\nFulcrum F\nRotation point\nLoad W\nLoad to be lifted\nEffort arm a\nEffort-to-fulcrum distance\nLoad arm b\nLoad-to-fulcrum distance\nTRICK - “P-F-W / a-b”\nP effort, F fulcrum, W load; a effort arm, b load arm.","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.3 Laws of Force & Motion\n• Principle of moments: P × a = W × b\n• Mechanical Advantage MA = W/P = a/b\nTRICK - “Pa = Wb; MA = W/P = a/b”\nLever ka two-formula pack.\n15. Lever Classes — Middle Person Rule\nClass\nMiddle\nMA\nExamples\n1st\nFulcrum\nCan be >1, =1 or <1\nScissors, see-saw, hammer,\npliers, beam balance\n2nd\nLoad\nAlways >1\nWheelbarrow, lemon squeezer,\nnutcracker, wheel & axle\n3rd\nEffort\nAlways <1\nTongs, ladder, plough\nTRICK - “1-F, 2-L, 3-E”\nClass number se middle element yaad karo.\nTRICK - “L-F-E / F-L-E / F-E-L”\n1st = Fulcrum middle; 2nd = Load middle; 3rd = Effort middle.\nEXAM TRAP - “2nd MA >1; 3rd MA <1; 1st Any”\nMechanical advantage trap.\n16. Final Exam Traps — 10 Red Flags\n• Balanced force\n a=0; object constant velocity se move bhi kar sakta hai\n→\n• Gravitational + magnetic = non-contact\n• 1st Inertia; 2nd F=ma; 3rd Action-Reaction\n• Action-reaction different bodies par act karte hain; cancel nahi karte\n• Impulse = FΔt = Δp; unit N·s\n• Momentum conserved only when net external force = 0\n• Limiting = max static; Rolling = least friction\n• Centripetal inward real; Centrifugal outward pseudo\n• Torque τ = rF sinθ; unit N·m\n• Lever: 1st F middle; 2nd L middle; 3rd E middle\nEXAM TRAP - “B-N / 1-2-3 / I / M / Friction / Circular / Torque / Lever”\nChapter ke 10 red flags ko isi sequence mein revise karo.\nMASTER MEMORY CHAIN — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\nMASTER - 1-Line Revision Chain\n“Force shape-side-state; SI N, CGS dyne, 1N=10^5 dyne\n types Contact/Noncontact + Balanced/Unbalanced; balanced a=0 but\n→\nconstant-v possible\n Newton 1 Inertia, mass measure; inertia R-M-D\n momentum p=mv; 2nd dp/dt\nF=ma; impulse I=FΔt=Δp N·s\n→\n→\n→\n→\n3rd equal-opposite different bodies; rocket-row-swim-recoil\n momentum conservation external F=0, m1u1+m2u2=m1v1+m2v2\n\n→\n→\nfriction F=μR; order Limiting>Static>Sliding>Rolling; rolling least; grip by grooves/sand/rough, reduce by\npolish/lube/bearings/streamline\n centripetal IN real F=mv²/r vs centrifugal OUT pseudo; level-road vmax= μsrg, banking\n→\n√\ntanθ=v²/rg\n torque τ=rFsinθ N·m; couple equal-opposite-parallel with gap\n lever Pa=Wb, MA=W/P=a/b; classes 1-F,2-L,3-E; 2nd\n→\n→\nMA>1,3rd<1,1st any.”\nTOP 5: Balanced force ≠ always rest | 1-2-3 Laws | Impulse=Δp | Limiting>Static>Sliding>Rolling | Lever 1-F, 2-L, 3-E.","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.4 Work, Power & Energy\nWORK, POWER & ENERGY — MEMORY TRICK SHEET\nGeneral Science · Physics · Chapter 4 · Work + Power + KE/PE + Conservation + Energy Conversion\nYe full notes nahi hai — source chapter ke work-sign rules, conservative/non-conservative force, power conversions, energy units,\nKE/PE formulae, free-fall conservation aur device-wise energy conversions ko fast exam recall format mein compress kiya gaya\nhai.\n1. Work — F-s-cosθ\n• Work tab hota hai jab applied force displacement produce kare\n• W = F·s·cosθ\n• Work scalar quantity hai; sign angle θ par depend karta hai\n• SI / MKS unit = Joule (J); CGS unit = erg\n• 1 J = 10^7 erg; 1 erg = 1 dyne × 1 cm\nTRICK - “Work = Force × Shift × cosθ”\nF-s-cosθ ko formula order mein yaad karo.\ncosθ hi positive/negative/zero sign decide karta hai.\nTRICK - “J to erg = ×10^7”\n1 J = 10^7 erg; reverse = 1 erg = 10^-7 J.\n2. Work Sign — + / 0 / -\nWork\nAngle\nSource Example\nPositive\n0°\n θ < 90°\n≤\nGravity on freely falling body\nZero\nθ = 90° or 270°\nLifting load sideways; circular motion\nNegative\n90° < θ < 270°\nGravity while lifting body upward\nTRICK - “Same Side + ; Right Angle 0 ; Opposite -”\nFalling body: gravity and displacement same direction\n + work.\n→\nLifting body: gravity opposite displacement\n - work.\n→\nEXAM TRAP - “Circular = 90° = Work ZERO”\nSource trap: force perpendicular to displacement in circular motion.\n3. Conservative vs Non-Conservative\nFeature\nConservative\nNon-Conservative\nWork depends on\nInitial + final positions only\nInitial/final + path\nPath\nIndependent\nDependent\nClosed path work\n= 0\n≠ 0\nSource examples\nGravitation, electrostatic, magnetic\nFriction, viscous\nTRICK - “Conservative = Coordinates Only + Closed Zero”\nPath independent; closed-path work zero.\nEXAM TRAP -\nSource notes list gravitation, electrostatic and magnetic force under conservative examples.\n4. Power — Work per Time\n• Power = rate of doing work","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.4 Work, Power & Energy\n• P = W/t\n• Scalar quantity\n• SI unit = Watt (W) = J/s\n• 1 HP = 746 W\n• 1 kW = 10^3 W; 1 MW = 10^6 W\nTRICK - “Power = Work / Time; 1 HP = 746 W”\n746 ko 1000 se confuse mat karo.\nTRICK - “kW = 10^3; MW = 10^6”\nKilo 3 zeros; Mega 6 zeros.\nEXAM TRAP -\n1 Horse Power = 746 W, not 1000 W.\n5. Energy — Capacity to Work\n• Energy = capacity to do work\n• Scalar quantity\n• SI unit = Joule\n• Commercial unit of electrical energy = kilowatt-hour (kWh)\nEXAM TRAP - “Work aur Energy dono Joule; Commercial Electricity = kWh”\nkWh energy unit hai, power unit nahi.\n6. Energy Unit Pack — erg / cal / kWh / eV\nUnit\nJoule Equivalent\nerg\n10^-7 J\nCalorie\n4.2 J\nkWh\n3.6 × 10^6 J\neV\n1.6 × 10^-19 J\nTRICK - “7-4.2-3.6-1.6”\nerg 10^-7 J; calorie 4.2 J; kWh 3.6×10^6 J; eV 1.6×10^-19 J.\nEXAM TRAP -\n1 kWh = 3.6×10^6 J; 1 eV = 1.6×10^-19 J.\n7. Forms of Energy — T-E-C-L-S-N-M\nForm\nMemory\nThermal\nHeat\nElectric\nFlow of electric current\nChemical\nChemical reactions\nLight\nSun / light sources\nSound\nSound sources / instruments\nNuclear\nFission or fusion\nMechanical\nKE + PE\nTRICK - “T-E-C-L-S-N-M”\nThermal-Electric-Chemical-Light-Sound-Nuclear-Mechanical.","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.4 Work, Power & Energy\nTRICK - “Mechanical = KE + PE”\nSource chapter ka direct definition.\n8. Kinetic Energy — Motion + v²\n• Kinetic energy = energy due to motion\n• KE = 1/2 mv²\n• Momentum relation: KE = p²/(2m) = 1/2 pv\n• Examples: wind mills; fired bullet\n• Kinetic energy always positive in source notes\nTRICK - “KE = Half m v-Square”\nVelocity double ho to v² relation yaad rahe.\nEXAM TRAP - “Same Momentum\n Lighter Mass = Higher KE”\n→\nKE = p²/(2m), so equal p par m kam\n KE zyada.\n→\n9. Potential Energy — Height vs Spring\nType\nFormula\nExamples\nGravitational PE\nU = mgh\nBody at height\nElastic PE\nU = 1/2 kx²\nStretched bow; compressed spring\nTRICK - “Height = mgh; Spring = 1/2 kx²”\nGravitational vs elastic PE formula pair.\nTRICK - “k = spring constant; x = extension/deformation”\nElastic-energy symbols lock karo.\n10. KE vs PE — Motion vs Position\nFeature\nKinetic\nPotential\nDepends on\nMass + velocity\nMass + height/configuration\nFormula\n1/2 mv²\nmgh or 1/2 kx²\nExample\nMoving bullet; running car\nDam water; stretched spring\nCan be zero?\nYes, v = 0\nYes, reference h = 0\nTRICK - “KE = Move; PE = Place”\nMotion vs position/configuration.\n11. Conservation of Energy — Total Constant\n• Energy neither created nor destroyed; only converted from one form to another\n• Total energy of isolated system remains constant\n• Ideal free fall: KE + PE = Total Mechanical Energy = Constant\nTRICK - “Energy Changes Form, Not Total”\nConservation law ka one-line core.","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.4 Work, Power & Energy\n12. Free Fall — Top/Mid/Bottom\nPosition\nKE\nPE\nTotal\nHighest\nMinimum (0)\nMaximum\nConstant\nMidway\n1/2 total\n1/2 total\nConstant\nLowest\nMaximum\nMinimum (0)\nConstant\nTRICK - “Top = PE; Bottom = KE; Mid = Half-Half”\nTop PE max/KE0; bottom KE max/PE0.\nEXAM TRAP -\nTotal mechanical energy stays constant in the source free-fall model.\n13. Energy Conversion — Opposite Pairs\nDevice\nConversion\nSolar cell\nSolar/Light\n Electrical\n→\nDynamo\nMechanical\n Electrical\n→\nElectric Motor\nElectrical\n Mechanical\n→\nMicrophone\nSound\n Electrical\n→\nLoudspeaker\nElectrical\n Sound\n→\nSitar\nMechanical\n Sound\n→\nBulb / Tube light / Heater\nElectrical\n Light + Thermal\n→\nBurning candle\nChemical\n Light + Thermal\n→\nBurning coal\nChemical\n Thermal\n→\nBattery / Electric cell\nChemical\n Electrical\n→\nEngine\nThermal\n Mechanical\n→\nPhotoelectric cell\nLight\n Electrical\n→\nTRICK - “Dynamo <-> Motor”\nDynamo: Mechanical\n Electrical.\n→\nMotor: Electrical\n Mechanical.\n→\nTRICK - “Microphone <-> Loudspeaker”\nMicrophone: Sound\n Electrical.\n→\nLoudspeaker: Electrical\n Sound.\n→\nEXAM TRAP - “Solar Cell + Photoelectric Cell = Light\n Electrical”\n→\nSource notes dono ko same conversion se link karte hain.\n14. Device Chain — C-B-E-S\nTRICK - “Chemical Battery\n Electrical Bulb\n Light/Thermal; Thermal Engine\n Mechanical”\n→\n→\n→\nBattery = Chemical\nElectrical.\n→\nBulb/heater = Electrical\nLight+Thermal.\n→\nEngine = Thermal\nMechanical.\n→\nSitar = Mechanical\nSound.\n→\n15. Source Worked Examples — Number Hooks\nExample\nSource Result\nF=40N, s=10m, θ=30°\nW = 346.41 J\nm=40kg, h=2m, t=4s\nWork 784 J; Power 196 W","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.4 Work, Power & Energy\nm=1500kg, v=72km/h=20m/s\nKE = 3 × 10^5 J\nm=40kg, h=5m, g=10m/s²\nPE = 2000 J\nTRICK - “346.41 / 196 / 3×10^5 / 2000”\nSource ke four worked-example answers ka recall pack.\n16. Final Exam Traps — 10 Red Flags\n• Work scalar; W = Fs cosθ; sign cosθ decides\n• Circular motion: force perpendicular displacement\n work = 0\n→\n• 1 J = 10^7 erg; 1 erg = 10^-7 J\n• Power = W/t; SI unit Watt = J/s\n• 1 HP = 746 W, not 1000 W\n• 1 calorie = 4.2 J; 1 kWh = 3.6×10^6 J; 1 eV = 1.6×10^-19 J\n• KE = 1/2mv²; equal momentum par lighter body has higher KE\n• Gravitational PE = mgh; elastic PE = 1/2kx²\n• Highest point: PE max/KE0; lowest: KE max/PE0\n• Dynamo M\nE; Motor E\nM; Microphone S\nE; Loudspeaker E\nS\n→\n→\n→\n→\nEXAM TRAP - “cosθ - Circle - J/erg - Watt - 746 - Units - KE - PE - FreeFall - Devices”\nChapter ke 10 red flags isi order mein revise karo.\nMASTER MEMORY CHAIN — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\nMASTER - 1-Line Revision Chain\n“Work W=Fs cosθ scalar; J SI, erg CGS, 1J=10^7erg\n sign: same +, 90° zero, opposite -; falling gravity +, lifting gravity -, circular\n→\nwork0\n Conservative path-independent/closed0 vs Non-conservative path-dependent\n Power P=W/t scalar, Watt=J/s, HP746W,\n→\n→\nkW10^3, MW10^6\n Energy capacity-to-work; commercial kWh; units erg10^-7J, cal4.2J, kWh3.6×10^6J, eV1.6×10^-19J\n forms T-E-\n→\n→\nC-L-S-N-M; Mechanical=KE+PE\n KE=1/2mv²=p²/2m=1/2pv; same p lighter mass higher KE\n PE: height mgh, spring1/2kx²\n\n→\n→\n→\nconservation: total isolated constant; free fall Top PE, Mid half-half, Bottom KE\n Conversions: Solar/Photoelectric Light\nElectrical;\n→\n→\nDynamo M\nE <-> Motor E\nM; Microphone S\nE <-> Loudspeaker E\nS; Sitar M\nS; Battery C\nE; Engine T\nM; Candle C\nL+T.”\n→\n→\n→\n→\n→\n→\n→\n→\nTOP 5: Circular work=0 | HP=746 W | kWh=3.6×10^6 J | Equal momentum: lighter\n higher KE | Dynamo<->Motor &\n→\nMicrophone<->Loudspeaker.","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.5 Gravitation\nGRAVITATION — MEMORY TRICK SHEET\nGeneral Science · Physics · Chapter 5 · Newton Law + G/g + Kepler + Satellites + Escape Velocity + Mass/Weight\nYe full notes nahi hai — source chapter ke G vs g, g-variation, Kepler laws, orbital/escape velocity, geostationary-polar satellites\naur mass-weight traps ko fast exam-recall format mein compress kiya gaya hai.\n1. Gravitation — Newton + Cavendish\n• Gravitation = universe ke kisi bhi do bodies ke beech attraction force\n• Universal law formulated by Isaac Newton\n• Universal gravitational constant G experimentally determined by Henry Cavendish in 1798\nTRICK - “Newton Law; Cavendish G-1798”\nNewton = law.\nCavendish = G ka experimental determination.\nEXAM TRAP -\nLaw aur G-determination ke scientists ko interchange mat karna.\n2. Universal Law — Product Up, Distance-Square Down\n• F m1m2 / r²\n∝\n• F = G(m1m2)/r²\n• Force masses ke product ke directly proportional\n• Centres ke distance ke square ke inversely proportional\nTRICK - “Mass Product UP; r² DOWN”\nm1m2 numerator; r² denominator.\n3. G vs g — Fixed vs Variable\nFeature\nG\ng\nMeaning\nUniversal gravitational constant\nGravitational acceleration\nValue\n6.67 × 10^-11 N·m²/kg²\nEarth near surface\n 9.8 m/s²\n≈\nNature\nUniversal / fixed\nChanges with location\nDepends on location?\nNo\nYes\nTRICK - “Capital G = Global Fixed; small g = Geography Changes”\nG everywhere same.\ng location ke saath change hota hai.\nEXAM TRAP - “G = 6.67 × 10^-11; g = 9.8”\nValue pair.\n4. g Formula + Core Values\n• g = GMe/Re²\n• Earth surface\n 9.8 m/s²\n≈\n• Moon surface\n 1/6 of Earth\n 1.63 m/s²\n≈\n≈\n• Earth centre = 0\n• g falling body ke mass par depend nahi karta\nTRICK - “Earth 9.8; Moon 1/6; Centre 0”\nThree-value chain.","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.5 Gravitation\nEXAM TRAP -\ng does not depend on mass of falling body.\n5. g Variation — P-E-Up-Down-Centre\nLocation\ng\nWhy / Memory\nPoles\nMaximum\nCloser to centre + rotation effect minimum\nEquator\nMinimum\nFarther + rotational effect maximum\nAltitude increases\nDecreases\nDistance from centre increases\nDepth increases\nDecreases\nEffective attracting mass reduces\nEarth centre\n0\nNet pull cancels\nTRICK - “Pole Peak; Equator Low; Up Down Both g Down; Centre Zero”\nP > E; altitude\n g\n; depth\n g\n; centre 0.\n↑\n↓\n↑\n↓\nTRICK - “Altitude\n 1/r²; Depth\n Linear Down”\n≈\n≈\nSource recall: altitude with distance inverse-square; depth approx linearly to zero.\nEXAM TRAP -\ng maximum poles, minimum equator — reverse mat karna.\n6. Kepler’s 3 Laws — E-A-T\nLaw\nName\nKey Point\n1\nLaw of Orbits\nElliptical orbit; Sun at one focus\n2\nLaw of Equal Areas\nEqual areas in equal times; faster perihelion,\nslower aphelion\n3\nHarmonic Law\nT² a³\n∝\nTRICK - “1-Ellipse, 2-Areas, 3-T²a³”\nKepler = E-A-T.\nTRICK - “Peri = Fast; Aphe = Slow”\nSecond-law speed trap.\nEXAM TRAP - “Sun at FOCUS, not Centre”\nLaw 1 ka common MCQ trap.\nTRICK - “Law 2 = Constant Areal Speed = Angular Momentum Link”\nSource notes Law 2 ko conservation of angular momentum se connect karte hain.\n7. Satellite Basics — Free Fall + Weightlessness\n• Satellite = body revolving around a planet\n• Moon = natural satellite; INSAT/GPS-type spacecraft = artificial satellites\n• Orbital velocity near Earth Vo = (gR)\n 8 km/s\n√\n≈\n• Satellite in orbit continuously free fall mein hota hai\n• Apparent weight in orbit = 0; mass unchanged\nTRICK - “Orbit = Free Fall; Weight 0, Mass Same”\nWeightlessness means apparent weight zero, mass zero nahi.","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.5 Gravitation\nEXAM TRAP -\nMass unchanged during orbital weightlessness.\n8. Geostationary — 24 / 36,000 / Equator / W\nE\n→\nFeature\nGeostationary\nTime period\n24 h\nHeight\n 36,000 km above Earth\n≈\nOrbital plane\nEquatorial\nDirection\nWest\n East\n→\nAppearance\nStationary relative to Earth surface\nUses\nTV, communication, weather monitoring\nIndian example\nINSAT series\nTRICK - “Geo = 24-36-E-WE”\n24 h, 36,000 km, Equator, West-to-East.\nEXAM TRAP - “36,000 not 3,600”\nZero count ka direct exam trap.\n9. Geostationary vs Polar\nFeature\nGeostationary\nPolar\nOrbit\nEquatorial\nNear poles\nHeight\n 36,000 km\n≈\n 500-800 km\n≈\nPeriod\n24 h\nMuch shorter\nMain use\nCommunication/weather/TV\nRemote sensing/mapping/surveillance\nTRICK - “GEO = Communication; POLAR = Picture/Mapping”\nUse-based distinction.\n10. Orbital vs Escape Velocity — 8 vs 11.2\nQuantity\nFormula\nEarth Value\nOrbital velocity Vo\n(gR)\n√\n 8 km/s\n≈\nEscape velocity Ve\n(2gR)\n√\n11.2 km/s\nRelation\nVe = 2 Vo\n√\n 41% higher\n≈\nTRICK - “Orbit 8; Escape 11.2; Ve = 2 Vo”\n√\n8\n 11.2 relation pack.\n→\nTRICK - “Escape Earth 11.2; Moon 2.35”\nEarth-Moon escape value pair.\nEXAM TRAP -\nEscape velocity projectile mass par depend nahi karta; planet mass/radius par karta hai.","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.5 Gravitation\n11. Escape Speed Meaning\n• Minimum launch speed needed to escape planet gravitational field permanently without further propulsion\n• Below 11.2 km/s from Earth: body eventually returns\n• At 11.2 km/s: just escapes\n• Above it: escapes with excess kinetic energy\nTRICK - “Below Return; Equal Just Escape; Above Extra KE”\nEarth escape threshold logic.\n12. Mass vs Weight — m vs mg\nFeature\nMass\nWeight\nMeaning\nAmount of matter / intrinsic property\nGravitational force\nFormula\n—\nW = mg\nSI unit\nkg\nnewton N\nType\nScalar\nVector\nChanges with location?\nNo\nYes\nEarth centre\nSame\n0\nMoon\nSame\n1/6 Earth weight\nTRICK - “Mass = Matter = Same; Weight = mg = g Changes”\nMass fixed; weight location dependent.\nTRICK - “Moon: Mass Same, Weight 1/6”\nClassic exam pair.\nEXAM TRAP -\nWeightlessness = apparent weight 0, mass 0 nahi.\n13. Balance Trap — Spring vs Beam\n• Spring balance measures weight\n• Beam balance compares mass\nEXAM TRAP - “Spring = Weight; Beam = Mass”\nInstrument pair.\n14. Formula + Value Flash Table\nItem\nRecall\nG\n6.67 × 10^-11 N·m²/kg²\ng Earth\n9.8 m/s²\ng Moon\n 1.63 m/s²\n g/6\n≈\n≈\ng Earth centre\n0\nVo Earth\n 8 km/s\n≈\nVe Earth\n11.2 km/s\nVe Moon\n2.35 km/s\nGeo height / period\n 36,000 km / 24 h\n≈\nNewton gravitation\nF = Gm1m2/r²\nWeight\nW = mg\nKepler 3\nT² a³\n∝\nOrbital / Escape\nVo= gR; Ve= 2gR= 2Vo\n√\n√\n√","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.5 Gravitation\nTRICK - “6.67e-11 / 9.8 / 8 / 11.2 / 2.35 / 36k / 24”\nChapter ka number-chain revision.\n15. Final Exam Traps — 10 Red Flags\n• Newton gave law; Cavendish determined G in 1798\n• G fixed universal; g varies with location\n• g poles maximum, equator minimum, centre zero\n• Mass same everywhere; weight changes with g\n• Kepler 1 ellipse; Sun at one focus, not centre\n• Kepler 2 equal areas/equal times; perihelion faster, aphelion slower\n• Satellite orbit = continuous free fall; apparent weight zero, mass same\n• Geostationary = 24 h,\n36,000 km, equatorial, West\nEast\n≈\n→\n• Orbital\n8 km/s; Escape Earth 11.2 km/s; Ve= 2Vo\n≈\n√\n• Escape velocity independent of projectile mass\nEXAM TRAP - “Newton/Cavendish - G/g - P/E/C - Mass/Weight - K1/K2 - Satellite - Geo - 8/11.2 - Mass-\nindependent”\nChapter ke 10 highest-yield traps isi chain mein revise karo.\nMASTER MEMORY CHAIN — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\nMASTER - 1-Line Revision Chain\n“Newton gravitation law F=Gm1m2/r²; Cavendish G-1798; G=6.67×10^-11 fixed vs g=9.8 variable\n g = GMe/Re²; Moon g\n1/6, centre0;\n→\n≈\n\nPole Peak, Equator Low, altitude\ng\n, depth\ng\n\n Kepler E-A-T: 1 ellipse Sun focus, 2 equal areas/peri fast-aphe slow, 3 T² a³; law2\n↑↓\n↑↓→\n∝\nangular-momentum link\n satellite = continuous free fall; Vo= gR\n8 km/s; weightlessness apparent weight0, mass same\n\n→\n√\n≈\n→\nGeostationary 24h + 36,000km + equator + West\nEast + TV/communication/weather; Polar 500-800km + remote sensing/mapping\n\n→\n→\nEscape Ve= 2gR= 2Vo; Earth11.2, Moon2.35; below-return/equal-just-escape/above-extra-KE; independent of projectile mass\n Mass\n√\n√\n→\n\nmatter scalar kg same; Weight W=mg vector N varies; centre weight0, Moon weight1/6; Spring balance weight, Beam balance mass.”\nTOP 5: G fixed vs g variable | Pole max-Equator min-Centre 0 | Kepler 1/2/3 = Ellipse/Areas/T² a³ | Geo 24h-36,000km | Orbit 8 vs\n∝\nEscape 11.2 and Ve= 2Vo.\n√","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.6 Mechanical Properties of Matter\nMECHANICAL PROPERTIES OF MATTER — MEMORY TRICK\nSHEET\nGeneral Science · Physics · Chapter 6 · Elasticity + Pressure + Fluids + Flotation + Surface Tension + Viscosity + Bernoulli\nYe full notes nahi hai — source chapter ke stress-strain, elastic moduli, atmospheric/fluid pressure, Pascal, Archimedes, surface\ntension, capillarity, viscosity aur Bernoulli ke high-memory facts ko exam-recall tricks mein compress kiya gaya hai.\n1. Matter States — S-L-G-P-B\nState\nMechanical Hook\nSolid\nElasticity; definite shape + volume\nLiquid\nPressure, flotation, surface tension, capillarity, viscosity\nGas\nAtmospheric pressure\nPlasma\n4th state\nBose-Einstein Condensate\n5th state\nTRICK - “S-L-G-P-B = Solid-Liquid-Gas-Plasma-BEC”\nPlasma = 4th; Bose-Einstein Condensate = 5th.\nEXAM TRAP -\nPlasma 4th, BEC 5th — order ulta mat karna.\n2. Stress vs Strain — Unit vs No Unit\n• Stress = restoring force per unit area; Stress = F/A\n• Stress SI unit = N/m² = Pa\n• Strain = change in dimension / original dimension\n• Strain dimensionless hai; no unit\nStress Type\nMeaning\nLongitudinal-Compressive\nLength decreases\nLongitudinal-Tensile\nLength increases\nNormal Stress\nForce perpendicular to surface\nShear Stress\nForce tangential to surface\nStrain Type\nFormula\nMeaning\nLongitudinal / Linear\nΔL/L\nLength change\nVolumetric\nΔV/V\nVolume change\nShearing\nΔx/L\nAngular deformation\nEXAM TRAP - “Stress has Pa; Strain has NO unit”\nDono ka sabse common exam confusion.\n3. Hooke + Elastic Moduli — Y-β-η\n• Hooke’s Law: elastic limit ke andar Stress Strain\n∝\n• Elastic modulus E = Stress/Strain\n• Modulus unit = N/m²; dimension = [ML^-1T^-2]\n• Law named after Robert Hooke\nModulus\nRatio / Formula\nBest Memory\nYoung’s Y\nLongitudinal Stress / Longitudinal Strain; Y =\nMgL/(πr²ΔL)\nLength\nBulk β\nNormal Stress / Volumetric Strain; β = -PV/ΔV\nVolume\nRigidity η\nShear Stress / Shear Strain; η = F/(Aθ) = FL/(AΔx)\nShape\nTRICK - “Y = Length; β = Volume; η = Shape”\nY-β-η ko Length-Volume-Shape ke saath lock karo.","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.6 Mechanical Properties of Matter\nTRICK - “Liquids/Gases = Bulk Only”\nSource ke mutabik liquids aur gases ke paas Young’s ya rigidity modulus nahi.\nEXAM TRAP -\nBulk modulus mein negative sign compression ke under volume decrease ki wajah se.\n4. Thrust vs Pressure\nFeature\nThrust\nPressure\nMeaning\nForce perpendicular to surface\nThrust per unit area\nFormula\n—\nP = F/A\nSI Unit\nN\nPa = N/m²\nTRICK - “Thrust = Force; Pressure = Force/Area”\nPressure area ke saath inverse effect dikhata hai for same force.\n5. Atmospheric Pressure — Barometer + Altitude\n• 1 atm\n 10^5 Pa\n≈\n• Atmospheric pressure measured by barometer\n• Altitude badhne par atmospheric pressure decreases\nBarometer Trend\nWeather\nFalls suddenly\nStorm\nFalls slowly\nRain\nRises slowly\nClear weather\nTRICK - “Sudden Fall = Storm; Slow Fall = Rain; Slow Rise = Clear”\nBarometer weather chain.\nTRICK - “High Altitude: Pressure\n + Boiling Point\n + Cooking Time\n”\n↓\n↓\n↑\nPressure cooker opposite: pressure\n\n boiling point\n\n cooking faster.\n↑→\n↑→\nEXAM TRAP -\nHigh altitude par pressure aur boiling point dono decrease.\n6. Density + Relative Density\nQuantity\nFormula / Value\nExam Hook\nDensity ρ\nM/V\nSI kg/m³\nRelative Density\nρsubstance / ρwater at 4°C\nNo unit; hydrometer\nPure Water\n10³ kg/m³\nMaximum density at 4°C\nIce\n0.9 g/cm³\nLess dense than water\nTRICK - “Water = 1000 + Max at 4°C; Ice = 0.9”\nDensity number chain.\nEXAM TRAP -\nRelative density has no unit; source notes hydrometer as instrument.\n7. Hydrostatic Pressure + Pascal\n• Pressure at depth h: P = hρg\n• Total pressure: Ptotal = Pa + hρg","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.6 Mechanical Properties of Matter\n• Source example at 10 m water depth: total\n 2.01×10^5 Pa\n 2 atm\n≈\n≈\nTRICK - “Depth\n\n Pressure\n : hρg”\n↑→\n↑\nHydrostatic pressure formula.\n• Pascal’s Law: enclosed fluid par applied pressure equally every direction transmit hota hai\n• Applications: Hydraulic Lift, Hydraulic Brakes, Hydraulic Press\nTRICK - “Pascal = Lift-Brake-Press”\nHydraulic trio.\nEXAM TRAP -\nHydraulic lift/brakes/press = Pascal, Bernoulli nahi.\n8. Archimedes + Flotation\n• Immersed body experiences upward buoyant force equal to weight of displaced fluid\n• Buoyant force / upthrust = weight of displaced fluid\n• Apparent weight in fluid = actual weight - buoyant force\n• Applications: life preservers, submarines, lactometers\nCondition\nResult\nExamples\nWeight > Buoyant force\nSinks\nIron nail\nWeight = Buoyant force\nEquilibrium / submerged float\nSubmarine, neutral body\nWeight < Buoyant force\nRises / partly floats\nShip, wood block\nTRICK - “W>B Sink; W=B Balance; W<B Rise”\nFlotation condition chain.\nTRICK - “Archimedes = Upthrust = Displaced Fluid Weight”\nPrinciple ka one-line core.\n9. Floating Ice — 1/10 Up, 9/10 Down\n• Floating ice ka about 1/10 water ke above aur 9/10 submerged hota hai\n• Floating ice completely melt hone par water level unchanged rehta hai\n• Lactometer aur hydrometer flotation principle par work karte hain\nEXAM TRAP - “Ice = 1 Up : 9 Down; Melt = Level Same”\nIceberg trap ko number ke saath yaad karo.\n10. Surface Tension — F/l\n• Surface tension = force per unit length in liquid surface plane\n• T = F/l = mg/l\n• Unit = N/m\n• Numerically equivalent surface-energy unit = J/m²\n• Surface energy = W/ΔA\nTRICK - “Surface Tension = Force/Length; Surface Energy = Work/Area”\nN/m vs J/m² pair.\nCondition\nEffect\nTemperature increases\nSurface tension decreases\nCritical temperature\nSurface tension = 0\nSoap added\nSurface tension decreases\nKerosene spread\nSurface tension decreases","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.6 Mechanical Properties of Matter\nTRICK - “Heat\n\n ST\n; Critical\n 0”\n↑→\n↓\n→\nTemperature trend.\n• Wet brush hairs stick together\n surface tension\n→\n• Needle can float on water\n surface tension\n→\n• Raindrops spherical\n surface tension minimizes surface area\n→\n11. Capillarity — Thin Tube, More Rise\n• Capillarity = narrow tube mein liquid ka rise/fall due to surface tension\n• Examples: plant roots/stems water transport, lamp wick oil rise, wetting, fine capillary movement\n• Capillary rise 1 / tube radius\n∝\nEXAM TRAP - “Radius Thin\n Rise High”\n→\nCapillary rise inverse to tube radius.\n12. Viscosity — Fluid Internal Friction\n• Viscosity adjacent fluid layers ke relative motion ko oppose karne wali internal friction hai\n• Property of both liquids and gases\n• F = -ηA(Δv/Δz)\n• η = FΔz/(AΔv)\n• SI unit = kg·m^-1·s^-1; also called poiseuille (Pl)\nTRICK - “Viscosity = Fluid Friction”\nSolids ki property nahi; liquids + gases.\nHigh\n Low Viscosity\n→\nOrder\nTypical order\nHoney > Water > Kerosene > Petrol > Gas\nTRICK - “H-W-K-P-G”\nHoney > Water > Kerosene > Petrol > Gas.\n13. Temperature vs Viscosity — Opposite Trend\nSubstance\nTemperature\n\n Viscosity\n↑→\nLiquids\nDecreases\nGases\nIncreases\nEXAM TRAP - “Liquid Low; Gas Grow”\nTemperature badhne par Liquid viscosity\n, Gas viscosity\n.\n↓\n↑\n14. Fluid Flow — Laminar vs Turbulent\nFlow\nNature\nStreamline / Laminar\nOrderly regular paths; usually low velocity\nTurbulent\nIrregular chaotic paths; above critical velocity\nCritical Velocity\nMaximum velocity up to which streamline flow persists\nTRICK - “Laminar = Lines; Turbulent = Trouble”\nOrdered vs chaotic.\n15. Bernoulli — Fast Flow = Low Pressure\n• Ideal incompressible fluid in streamline flow ke liye total energy per unit volume constant\n• P + ρgh + 1/2ρv² = constant\n• Based on conservation of energy","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.6 Mechanical Properties of Matter\n• Fast flow\n low pressure; slow flow\n high pressure\n→\n→\nTRICK - “Bernoulli = Fast LOW, Slow HIGH”\nVelocity-pressure inverse recall.\nApplication\nSource Logic\nAeroplane Wings\nFaster air above\n lower pressure\n lift\n→\n→\nVenturimeter\nNarrow section pressure difference\n flow speed\n→\nMagnus Effect\nSpinning ball pressure difference\n curve\n→\nTin Roof\nFast wind above\n low pressure\n upward lift\n→\n→\nTrain Platform Suction\nFast train lowers nearby pressure\nTRICK - “A-V-M-T-T = Bernoulli Applications”\nAeroplane - Venturimeter - Magnus - Tin roof - Train platform.\nEXAM TRAP -\nBernoulli applies to ideal incompressible fluid in streamline flow.\n16. Formula Flash\nQuantity / Law\nFormula / Unit\nStress\nF/A ; N/m²\nStrain\nΔL/L, ΔV/V, Δx/L ; no unit\nElastic Modulus\nStress/Strain ; N/m²\nPressure\nF/A ; Pa\nHydrostatic Pressure\nhρg\nDensity\nM/V ; kg/m³\nSurface Tension\nF/l ; N/m\nSurface Energy\nW/ΔA ; J/m²\nViscous Force\n-ηA(Δv/Δz)\nBernoulli\nP + ρgh + 1/2ρv² = constant\n17. Final Exam Traps — 10 Red Flags\n• Strain dimensionless hai; no unit\n• Plasma 4th state; Bose-Einstein Condensate 5th\n• Liquids/gases have bulk modulus, not Young’s or rigidity modulus\n• High altitude: pressure\n and boiling point\n, so cooking longer\n↓\n↓\n• Water maximum density at 4°C; ice less dense\n• Hydraulic lift/brakes/press = Pascal’s Law\n• Buoyant force = weight of displaced fluid\n• Floating ice melts\n water level unchanged\n→\n• Liquid viscosity\n with temperature; gas viscosity\n↓\n↑\n• Aeroplane lift + Venturimeter = Bernoulli applications\nEXAM TRAP - “Strain-P/BEC-Bulk-Altitude-Water-Pascal-Buoyancy-Ice-Viscosity-Bernoulli”\nChapter ke 10 core traps isi order mein revise karo.","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.6 Mechanical Properties of Matter\nMASTER MEMORY CHAIN — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\nMASTER - 1-Line Revision Chain\n“Matter S-L-G-P-B: Plasma4, BEC5\n Stress F/A Pa vs Strain ratio no-unit; stress types compress/tensile/normal/shear; strain\n→\nlinear/volume/shear\n Hooke stress strain; moduli Y-length, β-volume, η-shape; liquids+gases bulk only; β negative in compression\n→\n∝\n Thrust force, Pressure F/A; 1 atm\n10^5Pa; barometer sudden fall storm, slow fall rain, slow rise clear; altitude pressure\n +\n→\n≈\n↓\nboiling point\n\n Density M/V; water1000 max at4°C; ice0.9; Hydro P=hρg; Pascal = lift-brake-press\n Archimedes\n↓→\n→\nupthrust=displaced-fluid weight; W>B sink, W=B balance, W<B rise; ice1/10 up-9/10 down, melt level same\n Surface Tension F/l\n→\nN/m, Surface Energy W/ΔA J/m²; heat\n ST\n; capillary thin tube\nhigher rise\n Viscosity fluid friction, H>W>K>P>G; liquid temp\n\n↑\n↓\n→\n→\n↑\nviscosity\n, gas\n\n laminar orderly vs turbulent chaotic\n Bernoulli P+ρgh+1/2ρv² constant, conservation of energy; Fast flow LOW\n↓\n↑→\n→\npressure; Aeroplane-Venturi-Magnus-Tin roof-Train platform.”\nTOP 5: Stress Pa vs Strain no-unit | Y-Length β-Volume η-Shape | Pascal hydraulic trio | Ice melt level same | Liquid viscosity\n but Gas\n↓\nviscosity\n with heat.\n↑","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.7 Temperature, Heat & Thermodynamics\nTEMPERATURE, HEAT & THERMODYNAMICS — MEMORY\nTRICK SHEET\nGeneral Science · Physics · Chapter 7 · Scales + Thermometers + Heat + Expansion + State Change + Heat Transfer + Thermodynamic\nLaws\nYe full notes nahi hai — source chapter ke temperature-scale conversions, thermometer ranges, specific/latent heat, thermal\nexpansion, water anomaly, phase-change effects, heat-transfer methods aur thermodynamic laws ko fast exam-recall format mein\ncompress kiya gaya hai.\n1. Temperature vs Heat — Measure vs Transfer\nConcept\nCore Idea\nUnit / Hook\nTemperature\nHotness/coldness ka measure\nKelvin K; thermometer\nHeat\nTemperature difference ke kaaran transferred\nenergy\nJoule J; hot > cold\nThermodynamics\nHeat-mechanical energy relation\nP, V, T variables\nTRICK - “Temperature = Measure; Heat = Transfer”\nHeat naturally hotter body se colder body ki taraf flow karta hai.\nEXAM TRAP -\nTemperature aur heat same cheez nahi: temperature measure hai, heat transferred energy.\n2. Temperature Scales — C-F-R-K-Ra\nScale\nFreezing\nBoiling\nDivisions\nCelsius\n0°C\n100°C\n100\nFahrenheit\n32°F\n212°F\n180\nReaumur\n0°R\n80°R\n80\nKelvin\n273 K\n373 K\n100\nRankin\n492 Ra\n672 Ra\n180\nTRICK - “C-F-R-K-Ra = 100-180-80-100-180”\nScale order ke saath interval divisions lock karo.\nTRICK - “Universal Relation”\n(C-0)/100 = (F-32)/180 = (R-0)/80 = (K-273)/100 = (Ra-492)/180.\nTRICK - “60°F = 288.5 K”\nSource solved conversion example ka final answer.\n3. Fixed Temperature Facts — 0 / -40 / 273.16\nFact\nSource Value\nAbsolute zero\n-273.15°C = 0 K\nCelsius = Fahrenheit\n-40°C = -40°F\nFahrenheit = Kelvin\n574.25°F = 574.25 K\nFahrenheit = Reaumur\n-25.6°F = -25.6°R\nHuman body\n37°C = 98.4°F = 310.5 K\nTriple point of water\n273.16 K\nTRICK - “-40 = C and F Same”\nCelsius-Fahrenheit ka only equal reading source notes mein -40.\nEXAM TRAP - “Absolute 0K; Triple 273.16K”\nAbsolute zero aur triple-point pair.","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.7 Temperature, Heat & Thermodynamics\n4. Thermometers — Range Memory\nThermometer\nRange\nSpecial Use\nGas (Nitrogen)\n500°C to 1500°C\nVery high temperatures\nPlatinum\n-200°C to 1000°C\nWide range, precision\nThermocouple\n-200°C to 1600°C\nWidest listed range\nTotal Radiation\nAbove 800°C\nVery high temp, no contact\nAlcohol\nBelow -40°C; fp\n -115°C\n≈\nVery low temperatures\nClinical\n35°C to 42°C\nHuman body\nTRICK - “Clinical 35-42; Thermocouple -200 to 1600”\nSource ke two highest-frequency thermometer ranges.\nTRICK - “Alcohol for Very Low; Pyrometer No Contact”\nAlcohol preferred because mercury freezes near -39°C.\nTotal-radiation thermometer very high temperature without contact.\nEXAM TRAP -\nWidest listed thermometer range = Thermocouple, source table ke mutabik.\n5. Heat Units — J-cal-BTU\nItem\nValue / Unit\nSI heat unit\nJoule J\nCGS heat unit\nCalorie cal\nBritish unit\nBTU\n1 BTU\n252 cal\nMechanical equivalent\n1 cal = 4.186 J\nTRICK - “1 cal = 4.186 J; 1 BTU = 252 cal”\n4.186 aur 252 ko number pair mein yaad karo.\nEXAM TRAP -\nBTU = 252 cal, not 1000 cal.\n6. Specific Heat — Q = msΔT\n• Specific heat = unit mass ka temperature 1°C raise karne ke liye required heat\n• s = Q/(mΔT), hence Q = msΔT\n• SI unit = J/kg°C; CGS = cal/g°C\n• Water specific heat = 4186 J/kg°C = 1 cal/g°C\n• Source uses: hot-water bottle, crop frost protection, vehicle radiator cooling\nTRICK - “Specific = Q / mΔT; Water = 4186 = 1 cal/g°C”\nWater ka high specific heat applications se jodo.\nTRICK - “2 kg, 30\n100, s=300 => Q=4.2×10^4 J”\n→\nSource solved example answer.\n7. Latent Heat — State Changes, Temperature Same\n• Latent heat = state change during absorbed/released heat without temperature change\n• L = Q/m\n• SI = J/kg; CGS = cal/g\nType\nChange\nValue","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.7 Temperature, Heat & Thermodynamics\nFusion\nSolid > Liquid\n80 cal/g\nVaporisation\nLiquid > Gas\n539 cal/g\nTRICK - “Fusion 80; Vapor 539”\nVaporisation latent heat much larger than fusion.\nEXAM TRAP - “Phase Change = Heat Changes, Temperature Doesn’t”\nState-change ka most important conceptual trap.\nTRICK - “Steam Burn More Severe”\nSource reason: vaporisation latent heat 539 cal/g > fusion 80 cal/g.\n8. Calorimetry — Lost = Gained\n• Heat Lost by Hot Object = Heat Gained by Cold Object\n• Based on conservation of thermal energy in an isolated system\n• Used to determine unknown specific heats\n• Ideal assumption: surroundings ko heat loss nahi\nTRICK - “Hot Lost = Cold Gained”\nCalorimetry ka one-line equation.\n9. Thermal Expansion — α : β : γ = 1 : 2 : 3\nExpansion\nSymbol\nFormula\nDimension Change\nLinear\nα\nΔL/(LΔt)\nLength\nArea/Superficial\nβ\nΔA/(AΔt)\nArea\nVolumetric\nγ\nΔV/(VΔt)\nVolume\nTRICK - “α-β-γ = 1-2-3”\nβ = 2α; γ = 3α.\nTRICK - “Railway Gaps = Summer Expansion”\nThermal-expansion application.\nEXAM TRAP -\nα : β : γ = 1 : 2 : 3 is a direct high-frequency source trap.\n10. Anomalous Water — 0 to 4 Contracts\nTemperature\nBehaviour\n0°C > 4°C\nWater contracts; volume decreases\nAt 4°C\nVolume minimum; density maximum\nAbove 4°C\nWater expands; volume increases\nTRICK - “0\n4 Contract; 4 = Density Max; >4 Expand”\n→\nWater ka anomalous sequence.\nTRICK - “Pond: 4°C Water Sinks, Surface Can Freeze”\nDensest 4°C water sinks; lower water liquid reh sakta hai.\nEXAM TRAP -\nWater densest at 4°C, not 0°C.","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.7 Temperature, Heat & Thermodynamics\n11. Expansion Order — Gas > Liquid > Solid\n• Gases show greatest volume expansion: Gases > Liquids > Solids\n• Ideal gas volumetric expansion coefficient in source: γp = γv = 1/273.15 per °C\n• Idealized relation mein all ideal gases same coefficient show karte hain\nTRICK - “G-L-S = Greatest to Smallest Expansion”\nGas > Liquid > Solid.\n12. State Changes — Absorb vs Release\nProcess\nTransition\nHeat\nMelting\nSolid > Liquid\nAbsorbed\nFreezing\nLiquid > Solid\nReleased\nBoiling/Evaporation\nLiquid > Gas\nAbsorbed\nCondensation\nGas > Liquid\nReleased\nSublimation\nSolid > Gas\nAbsorbed\nDeposition\nGas > Solid\nReleased\nTRICK - “Up in State = Absorb; Down in State = Release”\nSolid\nLiquid\nGas direction absorbs; reverse releases.\n→\n→\nTRICK - “Sublime: Dry Ice-Camphor-Naphthalene-Iodine”\nD-C-N-I source examples.\n13. Pressure + Impurity Effects\nAction\nMelting Point\nBoiling Point\nIncrease pressure\nDecreases for ice\nIncreases\nDecrease pressure\nIncreases\nDecreases\nAdd impurity\nDecreases\nIncreases\nTRICK - “Pressure Cooker: P\n\n BP\n\n Faster Cooking”\n↑→\n↑→\nHigh altitude opposite: P\n\n BP\n\n slower cooking.\n↓→\n↓→\nEXAM TRAP - “Impurity: MP Down, BP Up”\nSalt lowers freezing/melting point in source notes.\nTRICK - “Triple Point = 273.16 K”\nSolid, liquid and vapour coexist in equilibrium.\n14. Heat Transfer — C-C-R\nMethod\nHow\nMedium / State\nExamples\nConduction\nDirect contact transfer\nMainly solids; also mercury\nHot pan handle, soldering iron\nConvection\nBulk particle motion\nLiquids + gases\nSea/land breeze, hot-water\ncirculation\nRadiation\nElectromagnetic waves\nNo medium; vacuum works\nSunlight, infrared heater\nTRICK - “Conduction Solid; Convection Fluids; Radiation Vacuum”\nC-C-R master rule.\nEXAM TRAP - “Sea/Land Breeze = Convection”\nRadiation nahi.","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.7 Temperature, Heat & Thermodynamics\nTRICK - “Thermos Cuts All 3”\nSilvered walls reduce radiation; vacuum reduces conduction + convection.\n15. Thermodynamics — PV = μRT\n• Thermodynamics = heat aur mechanical energy ka relation\n• System variables: pressure, volume, temperature\n• Ideal gas equation in source notes: PV = μRT\n• μ = number of moles, R = gas constant, T = absolute temperature\nTRICK - “P-V-T System; PV = μRT”\nSource notation μ for number of moles.\n16. Zeroth, First, Second — Z-F-S\nLaw\nCore Statement\nExam Key\nZeroth\nA and B each thermal equilibrium with C => A and\nB equilibrium\nThermometry basis; Fowler (1931)\nFirst\nHeat supplied = ΔU + work done\nΔQ = ΔU + ΔW; energy conservation\nSecond\n100% heat\nwork impossible; cold\nhot\n→\n→\nspontaneous flow impossible\nNo 100% efficient engine; refrigerator needs work\nTRICK - “Z = Zero Temp Difference; F = First Energy; S = Second Direction”\nZeroth = thermal equilibrium.\nFirst = conservation.\nSecond = direction/limits.\nTRICK - “Fowler 1931 = Zeroth”\nSource-specific scientist/year recall.\nEXAM TRAP - “Cold\n Hot Needs Work”\n→\nRefrigerator/AC external work consume karta hai.\n17. Formula + Number Flash\nItem\nRecall\nScale conversion\nC/100 = (F-32)/180 = (K-273)/100\nSpecific heat\nQ = msΔT\nLatent heat\nL = Q/m\nExpansion\nα:β:γ = 1:2:3\nFirst law\nΔQ = ΔU + ΔW\nIdeal gas\nPV = μRT\n1 calorie\n4.186 J\n1 BTU\n252 cal\nWater specific heat\n4186 J/kg°C\nFusion / Vaporisation\n80 / 539 cal/g","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.7 Temperature, Heat & Thermodynamics\n18. Final Exam Traps — 10 Red Flags\n• Phase change ke during temperature constant rehta hai\n• Radiation vacuum mein work karta hai; conduction/convection matter require karte hain\n• Sea breeze + land breeze = convection\n• Water maximum density at 4°C, not 0°C\n• Ice floats because its density liquid water se lower hai\n• Pressure cooker: pressure\n\n boiling point\n\n faster cooking\n↑→\n↑→\n• High altitude: pressure\n\n boiling point\n\n slower cooking\n↓→\n↓→\n• 1 BTU = 252 cal\n• Clinical thermometer = 35°C-42°C; Thermocouple = widest listed -200°C to 1600°C\n• Zeroth = Fowler/thermal equilibrium; First = conservation; Second = no 100% heat-to-work\nEXAM TRAP - “Phase-Radiation-Breeze-Water-Ice-Pressure-Altitude-BTU-Thermometer-Laws”\nChapter ke 10 major traps isi sequence mein revise karo.\nMASTER MEMORY CHAIN — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\nMASTER - 1-Line Revision Chain\n“Temperature measure vs Heat transfer hot\ncold\n scales C-F-R-K-Ra with divisions 100-180-80-100-180; -40 C=F; absolute0=0K;\n→\n→\ntriple273.16K; body37°C=98.4°F=310.5K\n Clinical35-42; Thermocouple -200 to1600 widest; Alcohol very low; total-radiation no\n→\ncontact\n Heat units J/cal/BTU: 1cal4.186J, 1BTU252cal\n Specific Q=msΔT, water4186; Latent L=Q/m, Fusion80 vs Vapor539; phase\n→\n→\nchange temp same; Calorimetry Hot Lost=Cold Gained\n Expansion α:β:γ=1:2:3; railway gaps; Water0\n4 contracts, 4°C density max,\n→\n→\nabove4 expands; expansion G>L>S\n State up absorbs, down releases; sublimation D-C-N-I; Pressure\n BP\n, altitude P\n BP\n,\n→\n↑\n↑\n↓\n↓\nimpurity MP\n BP\n; Triple273.16K\n Heat transfer C-C-R: conduction solids, convection fluids, radiation vacuum; sea/land breeze\n↓\n↑\n→\nconvection; thermos reduces all3\n Thermo PV=μRT; Zeroth equilibrium/Fowler1931; First ΔQ=ΔU+ΔW conservation; Second 100%\n→\nheat\nwork impossible and cold\nhot needs external work.”\n→\n→\nTOP 5: -40 C=F | Clinical 35-42 vs Thermocouple widest | Fusion80 vs Vapor539 | Water density max 4°C | C-C-R + Zeroth/First/Second law\nsplit.","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.8 Periodic & Wave Motion\nPERIODIC & WAVE MOTION — MEMORY TRICK SHEET\nGeneral Science · Physics · Chapter 8 · SHM + Pendulum + Waves + EM Spectrum + Sound + Ultrasonic + Echo + Mach\nYe full notes nahi hai — source chapter ke SHM/pendulum relations, wave types, EM-spectrum discoverers, sound characteristics,\nhearing ranges, speed-of-sound effects, echo/reverberation aur Mach-number traps ko fast exam-recall format mein compress kiya\ngaya hai.\n1. Periodic Motion vs SHM\nConcept\nCore Idea\nExamples\nPeriodic Motion\nRegular time interval ke baad repeat\nSwing, fan, musical vibrations, satellite motion\nSHM\nMean position ke about oscillation; acceleration\nmean ki taraf\nClock pendulum, spring-mass system\nTRICK - “Periodic = Repeat; SHM = Restore to Mean”\nSHM mein restoring acceleration hamesha mean position ki taraf hota hai.\nEXAM TRAP -\nSHM acceleration constant nahi hota; displacement ke saath vary karta hai.\n2. SHM Formula Pack — T-n-ω\nQuantity\nFormula / Meaning\nTime Period\nT = 2π/ω\nFrequency\nn = 1/T\nTime Period-Frequency\nT = 1/n\nAmplitude\nMaximum displacement from mean position\nPhase\nDirection/state of motion ko express karta hai\nTRICK - “T and n = Reciprocal Twins”\nn = 1/T. Higher frequency\n shorter time period.\n→\nTRICK - “Amplitude = Maximum Displacement”\nMean position se maximum displacement ko amplitude bolte hain.\n3. Simple Pendulum — T = 2π (l/g)\n√\n• Simple pendulum = heavy point mass (bob) + light inextensible string + fixed support\n• T = 2π (l/g)\n√\n• T depends on length l and gravitational acceleration g\n• T does NOT depend on bob mass\nTRICK - “Pendulum T = Length UP, g DOWN”\nl badhe\n T badhe.\n→\ng ghate\n T badhe.\n→\nEXAM TRAP - “Mass Changes? T Says NO.”\nBob mass ka time period par no effect.","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.8 Periodic & Wave Motion\n4. Pendulum Conditions — Up/Down/Satellite/Summer/Moon\nCondition\nEffect on T\nReason\nAltitude increases\nT increases\ng decreases\nDepth increases\nT increases\ng decreases\nSatellite\nT = ∞\nEffective g = 0 / weightlessness\nSummer\nT increases\nLength increases\nWinter\nT decreases\nLength decreases\nMoon\nT increases\ng_moon = g_earth/6\nBob mass changes\nNo change\nT independent of mass\nTRICK - “UP bhi T\n, DOWN bhi T\n”\n↑\n↑\nEarth surface se altitude aur depth dono directions mein g decreases, so T increases in source notes.\nTRICK - “Satellite = Infinity; Moon = Longer”\nSatellite mein pendulum oscillate nahi karta in source treatment.\nEXAM TRAP - “Max Pendulum T = 84.6 min”\nSource note: length equal to Earth radius ke liye maximum T = 84.6 minutes.\n5. Wave Motion — v = nλ\n• Wave = disturbance that transports energy from one place to another\n• v = λ/T = nλ\n• λ = v/n\n• Frequency source-set property hai; medium change par unchanged rehti hai\nTRICK - “Wave Triangle = v - n - λ”\nv = nλ; λ = v/n.\nEXAM TRAP -\nMedium change par speed aur wavelength change ho sakte hain, frequency nahi.\n6. Mechanical vs Electromagnetic Waves\nType\nParticle Motion / Medium\nExamples\nLongitudinal Mechanical\nAlong wave direction; medium required\nSound, seismic waves, tsunami, spring vibrations\nTransverse Mechanical\nPerpendicular; medium required\nTransverse spring motion\nElectromagnetic\nTransverse; no material medium required\nLight, radio, microwave, X-ray, gamma\nTRICK - “Sound = Longitudinal + Mechanical + Medium”\nVacuum mein sound travel nahi karta.\nTRICK - “EM = Transverse + Medium-Free”\nVacuum speed = 3 × 10^8 m/s.\nEXAM TRAP -\nMechanical waves need medium; EM waves do not.","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.8 Periodic & Wave Motion\n7. EM Spectrum Order — R-M-I-V-U-X-G\nWave\nDiscoverer (Year)\nKey Use\nRadio\nMarconi (1895)\nRadio & TV communication\nMicrowave\nHeinrich Hertz (1888)\nSatellites, wireless communication\nInfrared\nHerschel (1800)\nRemote control, fog photography\nVisible\nNewton (1666)\nHuman vision, bulb\nUltraviolet\nRitter (1801)\nForged-document detection, food preservation\nX-Ray\nRoentgen (1895)\nBone fractures, lung diseases\nGamma\nPaul Villard (1900)\nCancer treatment, nuclear structure\nTRICK - “R-M-I-V-U-X-G”\nRadio\n Microwave\n Infrared\n Visible\n UV\n X-ray\n Gamma.\n→\n→\n→\n→\n→\n→\nIs order mein wavelength decreases, frequency increases.\nTRICK - “Discoverers = M-H-H-N-R-R-V”\nMarconi-Hertz-Herschel-Newton-Ritter-Roentgen-Villard.\nEXAM TRAP - “Gamma Villard; Named Rutherford”\nMicrowaves generated by magnetron; visible\n 400 nm violet to 700 nm red.\n≈\n8. Sound Characteristics — L-P-Q-I\nCharacteristic\nMeaning / Relation\nExam Hook\nLoudness\nIntensity-linked; Loudness Amplitude²\n∝\nAlso falls approx 1/distance²\nPitch\nShrillness; Frequency\n∝\nHigher f = higher pitch\nQuality / Timbre\nDistinguishes same pitch & loudness\nTone one frequency; Note many\nIntensity\nSound energy per area per second\nUnit W/m²\nTRICK - “Loudness = Amplitude²; Pitch = Frequency”\nL-A² / P-f.\nEXAM TRAP - “Tone One; Note Many”\nSingle frequency vs multiple frequencies.\n9. Sound Levels — Quiet to Jet\nSource\nLoudness\nNormal breathing\n10 dB\nWhisper\n15-30 dB\nGeneral conversation\n30-60 dB\nBusy traffic\n70 dB\nJet plane\n140-150 dB\nTRICK - “10\n 30\n 60\n 70\n 150”\n→\n→\n→\n→\nBreathing\n whisper/conversation\n traffic\n jet.\n→\n→\n→\nEXAM TRAP - “85 dB+ Harmful”\nSource flags noise above 85 dB as harmful to human ears.","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.8 Periodic & Wave Motion\n10. Hearing Range — I-A-U\nRange\nCategory\nExamples / Use\n< 20 Hz\nInfrasonic\nSeismic waves, heartbeat\n20-20,000 Hz\nAudible\nAdult human hearing\n> 20,000 Hz\nUltrasonic\nBat, dog, dolphin, cat; SONAR; medical imaging\nTRICK - “I-A-U = Below20 / 20-20k / Above20k”\nInfrasonic-Audible-Ultrasonic.\nTRICK - “SONAR = Sound Navigation And Ranging”\nUses ultrasonic waves.\nEXAM TRAP -\nAdult hearing range = 20 Hz to 20 kHz.\n11. Animal Hearing — D-B-D-C-H\nAnimal / Entity\nSource Range\nDolphin\n150-1,50,000 Hz\nBat\n1,000-1,20,000 Hz\nDog\n15-45,000 Hz\nCat\n60-65,000 Hz\nChildren\nUp to 25,000 Hz\nHuman Adult\n20-20,000 Hz\nTRICK - “Dolphin Highest Listed 150k; Bat 120k”\nSource animal-range table ka fast recall.\n12. Ultrasonic Applications — M-S-D-I\n• Medical imaging: ultrasonography + echocardiography\n• SONAR: sea depth measurement\n• Detecting submarines and icebergs\n• Industrial cleaning and welding\nTRICK - “M-S-D-I = Medical - SONAR - Detection - Industrial”\nUltrasonic uses ka four-part chain.\n13. Speed of Sound — Solid > Liquid > Gas\nTRICK - “S-L-G = Fast to Slow”\nSpeed of sound: Solid > Liquid > Gas.\nMedium\nSpeed at 25°C\nAluminium\n6420 m/s\nSteel\n5960 m/s\nSea Water\n1531 m/s\nDistilled Water\n1498 m/s\nHydrogen\n1284 m/s\nAir\n340 m/s\nTRICK - “Aluminium Fastest Listed; Air\n 340”\n≈\nSource quick-number pair.","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.8 Periodic & Wave Motion\n14. Temperature, Pressure, Humidity Effects\nCondition\nEffect on Sound Speed\nTemperature increases\nSpeed increases; v T\n∝√\nPressure changes at constant T\nNo effect\nHumidity increases\nSpeed increases; moist air lighter\nTRICK - “T\n = v\n; Pressure@sameT = NO effect; Humidity\n = v\n”\n↑\n↑\n↑\n↑\nT-P-H trend.\nTemperature\nSpeed in Air\n0°C\n332 m/s\n22°C\n344 m/s\n25°C\n346 m/s\nEXAM TRAP -\nAt constant temperature, pressure has NO effect on sound speed.\n15. Echo vs Reverberation\nTerm\nMeaning\nSource Key\nEcho\nDistinct re-hearing of reflected sound\nPersistence 0.1 s; min distance 17.2 m\nReverberation\nProlonged persistence by multiple reflections\nHalls; megaphones/loudspeakers/stethoscope\ncontext\nTRICK - “Echo = Repeat; Reverb = Remains”\nEcho distinct repetition; reverberation prolonged persistence.\nEXAM TRAP - “0.1 s\n 17.2 m”\n→\nPersistence of hearing and source minimum echo distance.\n16. Mach Number — Sub / Super / Hyper\n• Mach Number = speed of body / speed of sound in same medium\n• Named after Ernst Mach\n• Sonic boom occurs when aircraft crosses sound barrier near Mach 1\nMach Range\nClass\nExample\nMach < 1\nSubsonic\nCommercial aircraft\n 0.8\n≈\n1 < Mach\n 5\n≤\nSupersonic\nFighter jets\nMach > 5\nHypersonic\nSpace shuttle during re-entry\nTRICK - “Sub <1; Super 1-5; Hyper >5”\nSource classification.\nEXAM TRAP -\nHypersonic = Mach > 5 in source notes.","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.8 Periodic & Wave Motion\n17. Formula + Number Flash\nItem\nRecall\nSHM time period\nT = 2π/ω\nPendulum\nT = 2π (l/g)\n√\nFrequency\nn = 1/T\nWave speed\nv = nλ\nWavelength\nλ = v/n\nSound vs temperature\nv T\n∝√\nEM speed vacuum\n3 × 10^8 m/s\nAdult hearing\n20-20,000 Hz\nPersistence of hearing\n0.1 s\nMinimum echo distance\n17.2 m\nMach number\nv_body / v_sound\n18. Final Exam Traps — 10 Red Flags\n• SHM acceleration always mean position ki taraf; constant nahi\n• Pendulum T bob mass par depend nahi karta\n• Altitude aur depth dono increase karne par T increases in source treatment\n• Satellite pendulum T =\n in source notes\n∞\n• Sound longitudinal mechanical; EM/light transverse\n• Medium change par frequency unchanged\n• Pressure at constant temperature has no effect on sound speed\n• Gamma discovered by Villard but named by Rutherford\n• SONAR uses ultrasonic waves\n• Echo min distance = 17.2 m; Hypersonic = Mach > 5\nEXAM TRAP - “SHM-Mass-UpDown-Satellite-Sound/EM-Frequency-Pressure-Gamma-SONAR-Echo/Mach”\nChapter ke highest-yield traps isi sequence mein revise karo.\nMASTER MEMORY CHAIN — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\nMASTER - 1-Line Revision Chain\n“Periodic repeats; SHM restores to mean, acceleration toward mean not constant\n T=2π/ω, n=1/T; pendulum T=2π (l/g), mass no\n→\n√\neffect; altitude\n/depth\n/summer/moon all T\n, winter T\n, satellite T=\n, source max84.6min\n Wave v=nλ, medium change\n↑\n↑\n↑\n↓\n∞\n→\nfrequency same\n Sound longitudinal mechanical medium-needed vs EM transverse medium-free speed3×10^8\n EM order R-M-I-\n→\n→\nV-U-X-G, wavelength\n frequency\n; discoverers Marconi-Hertz-Herschel-Newton-Ritter-Roentgen-Villard; gamma named Rutherford\n↓\n↑\n\n Sound L-P-Q-I: Loudness A², Pitch f, Tone one/Note many; >85dB harmful\n hearing I-A-U: <20 infra, 20-20k audible, >20k\n→\n∝\n∝\n→\nultrasonic; SONAR full form + ultrasound\n Sound speed S>L>G; Al6420, air\n340; T\nv\n, pressure@sameT no effect, humidity\nv\n\n→\n≈\n↑↑\n↑↑\n Echo distinct 0.1s / 17.2m vs Reverb prolonged\n Mach = body/sound; Sub<1, Super1-5, Hyper>5; sonic boom near Mach1.”\n→\n→\nTOP 5: Pendulum mass no effect | Sound longitudinal vs EM transverse | R-M-I-V-U-X-G + discoverers | Frequency unchanged across\nmedium | Echo 17.2 m + Hypersonic > Mach 5.","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.9 Electrostatics\nELECTROSTATICS — MEMORY TRICK SHEET\nGeneral Science · Physics · Chapter 9 · Charge + Coulomb + Field + Potential + Current + Resistance + Cells + Instruments\nYe full notes nahi hai — source chapter ke charge laws, electric field/potential, capacitance, current, Ohm law, resistance\ncombinations, heating effect, cells aur measuring instruments ke high-confusion facts ko fast exam-recall format mein compress\nkiya gaya hai.\n1. Charge — A-Q-C-R\nProperty\nRule\nAdditive\nQ = q1 + q2 + q3\nQuantisation\nQ = ne; e = ±1.6 × 10^-19 C\nConservation\nIsolated system ka total charge constant\nRepel / Attract\nLike repel; unlike attract\nTRICK - “A-Q-C-R = Add - Quantise - Conserve - Repel/Attract”\nCharge properties ka four-part code.\nEXAM TRAP - “Same Repels; Opposite Attracts”\nElectric force gravity ki tarah sirf attractive nahi — source notes attraction + repulsion dono batate hain.\n2. Coulomb’s Law — K q1q2 / r²\n• F = K(q1q2)/r²\n• K = 9 × 10^9 N·m²/C²\n• K = 1/(4πε0)\n• ε0 = 8.85 × 10^-12 C²/(N·m²)\n• Inverse-square law; source treats point charges\nTRICK - “Charge Product UP; Distance-Square DOWN”\nq1q2 numerator; r² denominator.\nEXAM TRAP - “K = 9×10^9; e = 1.6×10^-19”\nChapter ke two core constants.\n3. Conductor - Insulator - Semiconductor\nClass\nSource Behaviour\nExamples\nConductor\nFree electrons; charge flows easily\nSilver, Gold, Copper, Graphite, Sea water\nInsulator\nCharge does not flow easily\nRubber, Oil, Polythene, Pure water, Lead - as listed\nin source\nSemiconductor\nBetween conductor and insulator\nSilicon, Germanium\nTRICK - “Silver Best; Pure Water Insulator; Sea Water Conductor”\nSea water dissolved salts/ions ki wajah se conduct karta hai.\nEXAM TRAP -\nBest conductor in source = Silver, not Copper.","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.9 Electrostatics\n4. Electric Field — P\nN, Never Cross\n→\nItem\nRecall\nElectric field intensity\nE = F/q0; vector; N/C\nField-line direction\nPositive\n Negative\n→\nIntersection\nNever intersect\nClosed loops\nDo not form closed loops\nStrength\nCloser lines = stronger field\nConductor surface\nLines perpendicular to surface\nTRICK - “P-to-N; Never Cross; Perpendicular at Conductor”\nField-lines ka 3-rule pack.\nEXAM TRAP -\nField lines intersect karein to ek point par do directions ho jaayengi — source explanation.\n5. Potential + Capacitance — W/q and Q/V\nQuantity\nFormula\nNature / Unit\nElectric Potential\nV = W/q0\nScalar; Volt = J/C\nPotential Difference\nVa - Vb = W/q0\nVolt\nCapacitance\nC = Q/V\nFarad = C/V\nTRICK - “Potential = Work/Charge; Capacitance = Charge/Volt”\nV = W/q; C = Q/V.\nEXAM TRAP - “Farad, Not Volt/Ohm”\nPractical units: μF and pF.\n• Source solved potential example: W=2.25 J, q=12×10^-4 C\n V=1875 V\n→\n• Source solved capacitor example: C=1.0 μF, V=6.0 kV\n Q=6×10^-3 C\n→\n6. Electric Current — i = q/t\n• Current i = q/t\n• SI unit Ampere = C/s\n• 1 A\n 6.25 × 10^18 electrons per second\n≈\n• Conventional current: high potential\n low potential\n→\n• Electron flow direction conventional current ke opposite\n• Solids: free electrons; liquids: ions + electrons; semiconductors: holes + electrons\nTRICK - “Current Goes High\nLow; Electrons Go Opposite”\n→\nConventional current vs electron-flow direction.\nEXAM TRAP -\nCurrent direction aur electron direction same nahi.\n7. AC vs DC\nType\nDirection\nTypical Use\nAC\nPeriodically alternates\nHousehold supply, generators, transformers\nDC\nRemains constant\nBatteries, electronic circuits, charging\nTRICK - “AC Alternates; DC Direct”\nNaam hi direction rule yaad kara deta hai.","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.9 Electrostatics\n8. Ohm’s Law — V = IR\n• Proposed by George Simon Ohm in 1827\n• V I when temperature and other physical conditions remain constant\n∝\n• V = IR\nResistance Type\nSource Recall\nOhmic\nObeys V=IR; V-I graph straight; source example manganese wire\nNon-ohmic\nDoes not obey; V-I graph non-linear; diode, bulb\nTRICK - “Ohm 1827 + Constant Conditions”\nTemperature/physical conditions unchanged hona mandatory.\nEXAM TRAP -\nOhm law har condition mein valid nahi — source specifically constant physical conditions kehta hai.\n9. Resistance — Long More, Area More Less\n• R = ρl/A\n• R l\n∝\n• R 1/A\n∝\n• Resistivity ρ material property; unit Ω·m\nTRICK - “Length\n\n R\n; Area\n\n R\n”\n↑→\n↑\n↑→\n↓\nR = ρl/A.\n10. Series vs Parallel — Same I vs Same V\nFeature\nSeries\nParallel\nEquivalent R\nR1+R2+R3\n1/Req = 1/R1+1/R2+1/R3\nCurrent\nSame\nDifferent branches\nVoltage\nDifferent\nSame\nEquivalent R\nGreater than any individual\nLess than smallest individual\nSource Use\nFuse, Ammeter\nDomestic wiring, Voltmeter\nTRICK - “Series = Same I; Parallel = Same V”\nDomestic wiring = parallel.\nTRICK - “Series R Grows; Parallel R Shrinks”\nSeries equivalent > individual; parallel equivalent < smallest.\nEXAM TRAP -\nDomestic wiring series nahi, parallel.\n11. ρ - G - σ\nQuantity\nFormula\nUnit\nResistivity ρ\nρ = RA/l\nΩ·m\nConductance G\nG = 1/R\nSiemens S or Ω^-1\nSpecific Conductivity σ\nσ = 1/ρ\nΩ^-1 m^-1\nTRICK - “Resistance ka Reciprocal = Conductance; Resistivity ka Reciprocal = Conductivity”\nG=1/R; σ=1/ρ.\nTRICK - “Silver 1.60e-8; Copper 1.62e-8”\nSource resistivity table mein Silver slightly lower\n best conductor.\n→","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.9 Electrostatics\n12. Joule Heating + Power — H-W-P\nQuantity\nFormula\nHeating\nH = I²Rt joules = I²Rt/4.2 calories\nElectrical Work / Energy\nW = VIt = I²Rt = V²t/R\nPower\nP = VI = V²/R = I²R\nTRICK - “Heat I²Rt; Work adds t; Power removes t”\nH/W/P formulas ko family ki tarah yaad karo.\nEXAM TRAP - “1 kWh = 1 unit electricity”\nSource high-frequency recall.\n13. Bulb - Heater - Fuse - MCB\nDevice\nMaterial / Principle\nKey Detail\nBulb\nTungsten filament\nHigh resistivity; melting point 3422°C; N2 + Argon\nElectric Iron\nNichrome\nSource: 80% Nickel + 20% Chromium\nHeater\nCeramic plate + Nichrome\nResistance heating\nFuse\nLow-melting alloy\nSource: Cu + Sn + Pb; melts to break circuit\nMCB\nAutomatic protective device\nTrips and resettable\nTRICK - “Bulb = Tungsten; Heater/Iron = Nichrome”\nDo not swap.\nEXAM TRAP - “Fuse Melts; MCB Trips + Resets”\nProtection-device distinction.\n14. Electrolysis — Faraday\n• Current through acidic conducting solution causes ionic decomposition\n• Source associates law of electrolysis with Faraday\n• Applications: electric cell and electroplating\nTRICK - “Faraday = Electrolysis; Cell + Plating”\nChemical effect of electric current.\n15. Cell Basics — Primary vs Secondary\nType\nSource Energy / Rechargeability\nPrimary\nChemical\n Electrical; cannot be recharged\n→\nSecondary\nCharging: Electrical\n Chemical; Discharge: Chemical\n Electrical;\n→\n→\nrechargeable\nTRICK - “Primary = One-Time; Secondary = Recharge”\nRechargeability split.\n• EMF E = W/q; unit Volt\n• Terminal potential V = E - ir\n• Cell current I = E/(R+r)\n• Internal resistance source relation: r = (E/V - 1)R","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.9 Electrostatics\n16. Cell Voltage Memory\nCell\nEMF\nVoltaic\n1.08 V\nLeclanche\n1.46 V\nDry Cell\n1.46 V\nDaniell\n1.1 V\nBichromate\n2.0 V\nWeston Cadmium\n1.018 V\nLead Accumulator\n2.2 V\nAlkaline Accumulator\n1.35 V\nTRICK - “Dry/Leclanche = 1.46; Lead = 2.2”\nSource ke most important cell values.\nEXAM TRAP -\nLead accumulator 2.2 V; dry cell 1.46 V.\n17. Ammeter vs Voltmeter vs Galvanometer\nInstrument\nMeasures\nConnection\nIdeal R\nAmmeter\nCurrent\nSeries\n0\nVoltmeter\nPotential difference\nParallel\n∞\nGalvanometer\nCurrent detect + direction +\nmagnitude\n—\nMedium\nTRICK - “A = Series + Zero R; V = Parallel + Infinite R”\nAmmeter aur voltmeter ka complete exam pair.\nEXAM TRAP -\nAmmeter parallel nahi; voltmeter series nahi.\n18. Galvanometer Conversion — Opposite Additions\nConversion\nAdd\nGalvanometer\n Ammeter\n→\nLow resistance shunt in parallel\nGalvanometer\n Voltmeter\n→\nHigh resistance in series\nTRICK - “To A: Low Parallel; To V: High Series”\nLow-P / High-S.\nEXAM TRAP -\nGalvanometer\nAmmeter: low R parallel;\nVoltmeter: high R series.\n→\n→","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.9 Electrostatics\n19. Final Exam Traps — 12 Red Flags\n• Best conductor in source = Silver, not copper\n• Pure water = insulator; sea water = conductor\n• Field lines Positive\nNegative and never intersect\n→\n• Potential scalar; capacitance unit Farad\n• Conventional current opposite electron motion\n• Ohm law only at constant temperature/physical conditions\n• Series = same current; Parallel = same voltage; domestic wiring parallel\n• Bulb = Tungsten; heater/iron = Nichrome\n• Faraday = electrolysis in source\n• Dry/Leclanche = 1.46 V; Lead accumulator = 2.2 V\n• Ammeter series R=0; Voltmeter parallel R=∞\n• Galvanometer\nA: low-R parallel;\nV: high-R series\n→\n→\nEXAM TRAP - “Silver-Water-Field-Farad-Current-Ohm-Series-Bulb-Faraday-Cells-A/V-Galvano”\n12 exam traps isi chain mein revise karo.\nMASTER MEMORY CHAIN — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\nMASTER - 1-Line Revision Chain\n“Charge A-Q-C-R; e=1.6×10^-19C; Coulomb F=Kq1q2/r², K=9×10^9, ε0=8.85×10^-12\n Silver best conductor; pure water insulator, sea\n→\nwater conductor; semiconductor Si-Ge\n Field E=F/q vector N/C, lines P\nN, never cross/loop, conductor surface perpendicular\n\n→\n→\n→\nPotential V=W/q scalar Volt=J/C; Capacitance C=Q/V Farad\n Current i=q/t, 1A\n6.25×10^18 e/s, conventional High\nLow and\n→\n≈\n→\nelectrons opposite; AC alternates, DC constant\n Ohm1827 V=IR only constant conditions; R=ρl/A: length\nR\n, area\nR\n\n Series\n→\n↑↑\n↑↓→\nsame I and R grows; Parallel same V and R shrinks; domestic parallel\n ρ=RA/l, G=1/R, σ=1/ρ; Joule H=I²Rt, W=VIt=I²Rt=V²t/R,\n→\nP=VI=I²R=V²/R; 1kWh=1 unit\n Bulb tungsten; iron/heater nichrome; fuse melts, MCB trips/reset; Faraday electrolysis\n Primary\n→\n→\none-use vs Secondary rechargeable; E=W/q, V=E-ir, I=E/(R+r); Dry/Leclanche1.46V, Lead2.2V\n Ammeter series R0; Voltmeter\n→\nparallel R\n; Galvanometer to A low-R parallel, to V high-R series.”\n∞\nTOP 5: Silver best + pure/sea water split | Field P\nN never crosses | Series same I / Parallel same V | Tungsten vs Nichrome | Ammeter\n→\nseries R0 vs Voltmeter parallel R\n.\n∞","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.10 Magnetism\nMAGNETISM — MEMORY TRICK SHEET\nGeneral Science · Physics · Chapter 10 · Magnets + Materials + Magnetic Field + Lorentz Force + Fleming Rules + Flux + EMI\nYe full notes nahi hai — source chapter ke magnet types, magnetic-material classes, Oersted/Faraday timeline, field rules, Lorentz\nforce, Fleming rules, magnetic flux aur electromagnetic induction ke high-memory facts ko fast exam recall format mein compress\nkiya gaya hai.\n1. Magnet Basics — Dipole Always\n• Magnet iron aur uske ores ko attract karta hai\n• Every magnet has North + South pole; isolated magnetic monopole does not exist\n• Like poles repel; unlike poles attract\n• Freely suspended bar magnet ka North pole geographic North ki taraf point karta hai\n• Magnet ko cut karne par har piece phir N-S dipole ban jaata hai\nTRICK - “Magnet = N + S Forever”\nCut karo, phir bhi monopole nahi milta; smaller dipoles milte hain.\nEXAM TRAP -\nMagnetic monopole does NOT exist in source notes.\n2. Natural vs Artificial Magnet\nType\nSource Detail\nExamples\nNatural\nNature mein found; irregular; weaker\nLodestone / Magnetite = Fe3O4\nArtificial\nMan-made magnetic materials\nBar magnet, electromagnet, horse-shoe,\ncompass\nTRICK - “Lodestone = Magnetite = Fe3O4”\nNatural magnet ka direct equation.\nEXAM TRAP -\nFe3O4 yaad karo; FeO/Fe2O3 nahi.\n3. Magnetic Materials — Dia / Para / Ferro\nClass\nBehaviour\nExamples from Source\nDiamagnetic\nOpposite magnetisation; weakly repelled\nBismuth, Zinc, Gold, Copper, Silver, Diamond, Salt\nParamagnetic\nField direction; weakly attracted\nChromium, Sodium, Aluminium, Oxygen\nFerromagnetic\nDomain formation; strongly attracted\nIron, Nickel, Cobalt, Steel\nTRICK - “Dia = Door; Para = Passes Near; Ferro = Full Attraction”\nDia repels weakly; Para attracts weakly; Ferro attracts strongly.\nTRICK - “Ferro = I-N-C-S”\nIron - Nickel - Cobalt - Steel.\nEXAM TRAP -\nSilver/Copper = Diamagnetic; Aluminium = Paramagnetic.\n4. Oersted vs Faraday — 1820 vs 1831\nScientist\nYear\nDiscovery\nOersted\n1820\nCurrent-carrying conductor produces magnetic\nfield","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.10 Magnetism\nFaraday\n1831\nChanging magnetic flux produces induced\nEMF/current\nTRICK - “Oersted: Current\n Field; Faraday: Changing Flux\n Current”\n→\n→\n1820\n 1831 timeline.\n→\nEXAM TRAP -\nOersted aur Faraday ki discoveries interchange mat karna.\n5. Biot-Savart Law — I·l·sinθ / r²\n• B = (μ0/4π) · (IΔl sinθ / r²)\n• B I\n∝\n• B Δl\n∝\n• B sinθ\n∝\n• B 1/r²\n∝\n• μ0/4π = 10^-7 N/A²\n• Source unit of B = N/(A·m)\nTRICK - “Biot = I-L-sin / r²”\nCurrent, length aur sinθ numerator; distance-square denominator.\nTRICK - “μ0/4π = 10^-7”\nDirect numerical recall.\n6. Ampere’s Law\n•\n B·dl = μ0 I\n∮\n• Closed loop ke around magnetic-field line integral = μ0 × enclosed current\n• Source use: symmetric current configurations such as solenoid and toroid\nTRICK - “Ampere = Closed Loop + Enclosed Current”\nSymmetry problems ka source hook.\n7. Direction Rules — Right Hand + Cork Screw\nRule\nThumb / Motion\nFingers / Result\nRight Hand Palm\nThumb\n current I\n→\nFingers\n magnetic field B\n→\nMaxwell Right Hand\nThumb\n current\n→\nCurled fingers\n magnetic field\n→\nCork Screw\nCurrent upward\nField anti-clockwise\nCork Screw\nCurrent downward\nField clockwise\nTRICK - “Current UP = Anti-clockwise; DOWN = Clockwise”\nSource cork-screw rule.\nEXAM TRAP -\nUp/anti-clockwise and down/clockwise ko ulta mat karna.\n8. Lorentz Force — Moving Charge Only\n• Moving charge in magnetic field: F = qvB sinθ = q(v × B)\n• F q, v, B\n∝\n• θ = 0° or 180°\n F = 0\n→\n• θ = 90°\n F maximum\n→\n• Stationary charge v=0\n magnetic force zero\n→","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.10 Magnetism\nTRICK - “Lorentz = q-v-B-sinθ”\nCharge, speed, field aur angle.\nTRICK - “Parallel ZERO; Perpendicular MAX”\n0°/180°\n 0 force; 90°\n max.\n→\n→\nEXAM TRAP -\nStationary charge in magnetic field experiences NO magnetic force.\n9. Force on Current-Carrying Conductor\n• F = BIl sinθ\n• Force current aur magnetic field dono ke perpendicular act karta hai\n• θ = 90°\n force maximum\n→\n• Current parallel to B (θ=0°)\n force zero\n→\nTRICK - “Conductor Force = B-I-l-sinθ”\nLorentz qvB aur conductor BIl ko separate yaad karo.\n10. Fleming Rules — Left Motor, Right Generator\nRule\nUsed For\nThumb\nForefinger\nMiddle Finger\nLEFT\nMotor / force\nForce / motion\nMagnetic field B\nCurrent I\nRIGHT\nGenerator / induced current\nMotion of conductor\nMagnetic field B\nInduced current\nTRICK - “LEFT = MOTOR; RIGHT = GENERATOR”\nBoth rules mein Forefinger = Field, Middle finger = Current.\nTRICK - “F-B-I = Thumb-Fore-Middle on Left”\nLeft hand: Force - B - I.\nEXAM TRAP -\nMotor = Left; Generator/Dynamo = Right.\n11. Magnetic Field Lines\nProperty\nSource Recall\nOutside magnet\nNorth\n South\n→\nShape\nClosed continuous loops\nIntersection\nNever intersect\nUniform field\nParallel + equally spaced\nNeutral point\nMagnetic field = 0\nStrength\nCloser lines = stronger field\nTRICK - “N\nS Outside + Closed + Never Cross”\n→\nMagnetic-field lines ka 3-rule pack.\n12. Magnetic vs Electric Field Lines\nFeature\nElectric\nMagnetic\nDirection\n+\n -\n→\nN\n S outside magnet\n→\nClosed loops?\nNo\nYes\nIntersect?\nNo\nNo\nStart/end\nOn charges\nNo start/end; continuous","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.10 Magnetism\nTRICK - “Electric Open; Magnetic Closed”\nDono non-intersecting.\nEXAM TRAP -\nMagnetic field lines closed loops form karte hain; electric field lines nahi.\n13. Electromagnet — Soft Iron + Current\n• Wire coil around iron core + current\n electromagnet\n→\n• Core material = soft iron; easily magnetised and demagnetised\n• More turns + more current + better core material\n greater strength\n→\n• Magnetism only while current flows\n• Applications: electric bells, scrap-iron cranes, MRI machines, electric motors\nTRICK - “Soft Iron + Turns + Current = Strong Electromagnet”\nCurrent off\n magnetism off.\n→\n14. Magnetic Flux — Φ = BA cosθ\nItem\nRecall\nFormula\nΦ = B·A cosθ\nAngle θ\nBetween B and surface normal\nSI unit\nWeber Wb\nOther SI expression\nN·m/A\nCGS unit\nMaxwell\nConversion\n1 Wb = 10^8 Maxwell\nTRICK - “Flux = BA cos NORMAL-angle”\nθ surface ke saath nahi, surface NORMAL ke saath measured hai.\nEXAM TRAP - “Perpendicular to Surface = Flux MAX; Parallel to Surface = ZERO”\nBecause B aligns with normal at max flux.\nTRICK - “1 Weber = 10^8 Maxwell”\n10^8 direct conversion.\n15. Electromagnetic Induction — Changing Flux Needed\n• EMI = changing magnetic flux se conductor mein EMF + induced current generate hona\n• Faraday discovered EMI in 1831\n• Static magnet motionless near coil\n no induced EMF\n→\n• Condition = magnetic flux must change\nTRICK - “EMI = Change Flux, Not Just Field”\nStatic flux\n no induction.\n→\nEXAM TRAP -\nPresence of magnetic field alone is insufficient; flux must CHANGE.\n16. EMI Applications\nApplication\nRole\nDynamo / Generator\nMechanical\n Electrical\n→\nTransformer\nChanges AC voltage levels\nArtificial Pacemaker\nRegulates heartbeat\nOther source examples\nInduction cooktop; wireless charging","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.10 Magnetism\nTRICK - “G-T-P-IW = Generator-Transformer-Pacemaker-Induction/Wireless”\nEMI application chain.\nEXAM TRAP - “Generator = EMI; Motor = Magnetic Force”\nGenerator/right hand vs Motor/left hand.\n17. Formula + Number Flash\nItem\nRecall\nBiot-Savart\nB = (μ0/4π) IΔl sinθ / r²\nAmpere\nB·dl = μ0I\n∮\nLorentz Force\nF = qvB sinθ\nConductor Force\nF = BIl sinθ\nFlux\nΦ = BA cosθ\nμ0/4π\n10^-7 N/A²\nOersted\n1820\nFaraday\n1831\nFlux Conversion\n1 Wb = 10^8 Maxwell\nNatural Magnet\nLodestone = Magnetite = Fe3O4\n18. Final Exam Traps — 10 Red Flags\n• Lodestone = Magnetite = Fe3O4\n• Magnetic monopoles do not exist\n• Silver/Copper diamagnetic; Aluminium paramagnetic\n• Oersted 1820 = current\nfield; Faraday 1831 = changing flux\nEMI\n→\n→\n• Stationary charge has zero Lorentz magnetic force\n• Left hand = motor; Right hand = generator\n• Magnetic field lines closed; electric field lines not closed\n• 1 Weber = 10^8 Maxwell\n• Flux angle is measured with surface normal\n• EMI requires changing magnetic flux\nEXAM TRAP - “Fe3O4-Monopole-Materials-O/F-Stationary-L/R-Lines-Wb-Normal-Change”\nChapter ke 10 exam traps isi order mein revise karo.\nMASTER MEMORY CHAIN — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\nMASTER - 1-Line Revision Chain\n“Magnet always N-S dipole; like repel/unlike attract; cut\nsmaller dipoles; Lodestone=Magnetite=Fe3O4\n Materials: Dia weak repel\n→\n→\n(Bi,Zn,Au,Cu,Ag,Diamond,Salt), Para weak attract (Cr,Na,Al,O2), Ferro strong/domain (Fe,Ni,Co,Steel)\n Oersted1820\n→\ncurrent\nmagnetic field; Faraday1831 changing flux\nEMI\n Biot B=(μ0/4π)IΔl sinθ/r² with μ0/4π=10^-7; Ampere\nBdl=μ0I\n\n→\n→\n→\n→\n∮\nDirection: right-hand thumb current/fingers field; current UP anti-clockwise, DOWN clockwise\n Lorentz F=qvBsinθ: stationary0,\n→\nparallel0, perpendicularmax; conductor F=BIl sinθ\n Fleming LEFT motor F-B-I, RIGHT generator motion-B-current\n Magnetic lines\n→\n→\n\nN\nS outside, closed, never intersect; electric open, magnetic closed\n Electromagnet soft iron + turns/current strength; current off\n→\n→\nmagnetism off\n Flux Φ=BAcosθ, θ with normal, Wb; 1Wb=10^8Maxwell; perpendicular-surface max, parallel-surface zero\n EMI\n→\n→\nrequires changing flux; generator, transformer, pacemaker, induction cooktop, wireless charging; generator=EMI, motor=magnetic-\nforce.”\nTOP 5: Fe3O4 Lodestone | Dia/Para/Ferro examples | Oersted1820 vs Faraday1831 | Left Motor vs Right Generator | Flux angle with\nNORMAL + 1 Wb=10^8 Maxwell.","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.11 Light\nLIGHT — MEMORY TRICK SHEET\nGeneral Science · Physics · Chapter 11 · Reflection + Refraction + TIR + Mirrors + Lenses + Dispersion + Eye + Wave Optics +\nPhotoelectric Effect\nYe full notes nahi hai — source chapter ke mirror/lens sign traps, TIR conditions, VIBGYOR, human-eye defects, optical\ninstruments, wave-optics scientists aur photon/photoelectric-effect facts ko fast exam-recall format mein compress kiya gaya hai.\n1. Light Basics — EM + Dual Nature\n• Light = energy form; normally straight line mein travel karta hai\n• Material medium required nahi hota\n• Transverse electromagnetic wave\n• Dual nature: wave + particle behaviour\n• Vacuum speed = 3 × 10^8 m/s\n• Visible wavelength = 3900-7800 Å\nTRICK - “Light = No Medium + Transverse EM + Dual”\nVacuum speed 3×10^8 m/s; visible 3900-7800 Å.\nTheory\nScientist / Year\nSource Hook\nParticle\nDescartes 1637; Newton 1700\nReflection/refraction + particle behaviour\nWave\nHuygens 1678\nInterference, diffraction, polarisation\nTRICK - “D-N Particle; H Wave”\nDescartes/Newton = particle side; Huygens = wave side.\n2. Reflection — i = r + Coplanarity\n• Reflection = light ka original medium mein return after striking surface\n• Law 1: angle of incidence = angle of reflection\n• Law 2: incident ray, reflected ray and normal same plane mein\nTRICK - “Reflection = i = r + Same Plane”\nAngles normal se measured hote hain.\n3. Plane Mirror — V-E-S-L\nProperty\nRecall\nNature\nVirtual\nOrientation\nErect\nSize\nEqual to object\nSpecial\nLaterally inverted\nDistance\nImage behind = object distance in front\nTRICK - “Plane Mirror = V-E-S-L”\nVirtual - Erect - Same size - Lateral inversion.\nEXAM TRAP -\nPlane-mirror image real nahi hota.\n4. Spherical Mirrors — Concave vs Convex\nMirror\nNature\nMajor Uses\nConcave\nConverging\nShaving, dentist, torch/headlight, solar cooker\nConvex\nDiverging\nRear-view mirror, eyeglass, street lights","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.11 Light\nTRICK - “Rear-view = Convex; Shaving/Dentist = Concave”\nConvex wide field + diminished virtual image.\nConcave close object F-P ke beech enlarged virtual image.\nEXAM TRAP - “Convex Always V-E-D”\nVirtual - Erect - Diminished.\n5. Concave Mirror Image Chain\nObject Position\nImage Position\nNature\nInfinity\nF\nReal, inverted, highly diminished\nBeyond C\nBetween C & F\nReal, inverted, diminished\nAt C\nAt C\nReal, inverted, same size\nBetween C & F\nBeyond C\nReal, inverted, enlarged\nAt F\nInfinity\nReal, inverted, highly enlarged\nBetween F & P\nBehind mirror\nVirtual, erect, enlarged\nEXAM TRAP - “Concave Virtual ONLY F-P”\nObject between F and P\n behind mirror, virtual erect enlarged.\n→\n6. Mirror Formula — PLUS\nFormula / Sign\nRecall\nMirror formula\n1/v + 1/u = 1/f\nFocal-radius\nf = R/2\nMagnification\nm = -v/u\nConvex f\nPositive\nConcave f\nNegative\nObject distance u\nNegative in source sign convention\nTRICK - “MIRROR = PLUS”\n1/v + 1/u = 1/f.\nTRICK - “f = R/2”\nSource example: f=10 m\n R=20 m.\n→\n7. Refraction — Snell + Frequency Same\n• Refraction = transparent medium change par light deviation\n• Denser medium mein speed decreases and ray normal ki taraf bends\n• Frequency remains unchanged\n• Snell: sin i / sin r = constant = μ\n• μ = c/v\nTRICK - “Denser = Slower + Toward Normal; Frequency SAME”\nHigher refractive index = optically denser = lower light speed.\nEXAM TRAP -\nMedium change par frequency unchanged.\n8. Total Internal Reflection — D-R + i>c\n• Condition 1: light denser\n rarer medium\n→\n• Condition 2: incidence angle > critical angle\n• sin c = 1/μ\n• Applications: optical fibre, medical endoscope, diamond brilliance, mirage, shining air bubble/crack","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.11 Light\nTRICK - “TIR = Denser\nRarer + i > Critical”\n→\nD-R + i>c.\nTRICK - “Diamond = Small Critical Angle\n 24°”\n≈\nHigh refractive index\n TIR easily.\n→\nEXAM TRAP -\nTIR rarer\ndenser travel mein nahi hota.\n→\n9. Lenses — Convex vs Concave\nLens\nShape/Behaviour\nSign / Uses\nConvex\nThicker centre; converging\nPositive f; farsightedness, magnifying glass,\ncamera\nConcave\nThinner centre; diverging\nNegative f; myopia\nTRICK - “Convex = Converge = Positive; Concave = Cave-in = Diverge = Negative”\nPower sign bhi same: convex +, concave -.\n10. Lens Formula — MINUS\nFormula\nRecall\nLens formula\n1/v - 1/u = 1/f\nMagnification\nm = v/u = I/O\nPower\nP = 1/f (metre)\nUnit\nDiopter D\nTRICK - “MIRROR PLUS, LENS MINUS”\nMirror: 1/v + 1/u = 1/f.\nLens: 1/v - 1/u = 1/f.\nEXAM TRAP - “f = -20 cm\n P = -5 D”\n→\nNegative power = concave lens.\n11. Dispersion — VIBGYOR\nColour\nSource Exam Point\nViolet\nMost deviated; most scattered\nBlue\nStrongly scattered; sky appears blue\nGreen\nPrimary colour of light\nRed\nLeast deviated; least scattered\nTRICK - “Violet MOST; Red LEAST”\nDeviation aur scattering dono mein source same extreme pair deta hai.\nTRICK - “VIBGYOR: Violet\nRed = Deviation/Scattering Down”\n→\nViolet end high, red end low.\n12. Rainbow + Colour Mixing\n• Rainbow: refraction + TIR + refraction in water droplets\n• Sun observer ke behind hota hai\n• Primary colours of light = Red, Green, Blue\n• Primary colours of pigment/paint = Red, Yellow, Blue","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.11 Light\nMix\nResult\nGreen + Red\nYellow\nBlue + Red\nMagenta\nBlue + Green\nCyan\nRGB\nWhite\nTRICK - “RGB = Light = White”\nR+G=Yellow; B+R=Magenta; B+G=Cyan.\nTRICK - “Complementary: R-C, G-M, B-Y”\nRed-Cyan; Green-Magenta; Blue-Yellow.\n13. Rayleigh Scattering — Shorter More\nObservation\nReason\nViolet scatters most\nShortest visible wavelength side\nSky appears blue\nBlue strongly scattered + eye more sensitive to blue than violet\nSunrise/sunset red\nLong path scatters shorter wavelengths away\nDanger signal red\nRed scatters least\nTRICK - “Shorter λ\n More Scatter; Red = Least”\n→\nDanger signal red because red scatters least.\nEXAM TRAP -\nViolet scatters more than blue, but sky appears blue in source explanation.\n14. Human Eye — C-I-P-L-R-F-B\nPart\nFunction / Hook\nCornea\nTransparent front; light entry\nIris\nColoured opaque part; controls pupil size\nPupil\nControls amount of entering light\nEye lens\nAdjustable converging lens\nRetina\nReal, inverted image forms\nFovea / Yellow spot\nSharpest vision\nBlind spot\nZero light sensitivity\nCiliary muscles\nChange lens focal length\nTRICK - “Retina = Real + Inverted; Fovea = Finest; Blind = Zero”\nThree eye-image traps.\n• Least distance of distinct vision = 25 cm\n• Normal vision range = 25 cm to infinity\n• Power of human eye = 4 D as given in source\nEXAM TRAP -\nRetinal image real and inverted; brain interprets it upright.\n15. Vision Defects — M-H-P-A\nDefect\nSource Symptom\nCorrection\nMyopia\nNear clear; far blurred\nConcave lens\nHypermetropia\nFar clear; near blurred\nConvex lens\nPresbyopia\nOld age; near/far difficulty\nBifocal lens\nAstigmatism\nBlurred all distances; irregular curvature\nCylindrical lens\nColour blindness\nRed-green distinction problem; hereditary\nNo cure / no lens correction","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.11 Light\nTRICK - “M-H-P-A = Concave-Convex-Bifocal-Cylindrical”\nMyopia\nConcave; Hypermetropia\nConvex; Presbyopia\nBifocal; Astigmatism\nCylindrical.\n→\n→\n→\n→\nEXAM TRAP -\nColour blindness has no lens correction in source.\n16. Optical Instruments — Microscope vs Telescope\nInstrument\nSource Formula / Hook\nSimple Microscope\nConvex short-f lens; M = 1 + D/F, D=25 cm\nReflecting Telescope\nConcave mirror objective; bright magnified distant images\nAstronomical Telescope at ∞\nM = -Fo/Fe\nTelescope Setup\nObjective large Fo; eyepiece small Fe; larger Fo/Fe\n more magnification\n→\nTRICK - “Telescope: Objective LONG, Eyepiece SHORT”\nFo large; Fe small.\n17. Wave Optics — Young / Diffraction / Polarisation\nPhenomenon\nSource Recall\nInterference\nThomas Young 1801; bright constructive, dark destructive\nDiffraction\nBending at edges/obstacles; Fresnel nearby source, Fraunhofer parallel\nrays\nPolarisation\nVibrations restricted to one direction; proves light transverse\nTRICK - “Young = Interference 1801”\nSoap-bubble colours = common interference example in source.\nEXAM TRAP - “Polarisation PROVES Transverse”\nNot longitudinal.\n18. Photoelectric Effect + Photon Theory\nScientist / Year\nContribution\nPlanck 1900\nQuantum/photon theory; E = hν\nEinstein 1905\nPhotoelectric effect explanation\nEinstein Nobel 1921\nFor photoelectric effect\n• Photoelectric effect: sufficient-frequency light falls on metal\n electrons emitted\n→\n• Threshold frequency = minimum frequency needed\n• Kinetic energy of emitted electrons depends on frequency, not intensity — source exam point\n• Photon energy E = hν\n• Photon momentum p = h/λ\n• ν = c/λ\nTRICK - “Planck 1900 Photon; Einstein 1905 Photoelectric; Nobel 1921”\nP-E-N timeline.\nEXAM TRAP -\nEinstein’s 1921 Nobel Prize source says photoelectric effect, not relativity.","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.11 Light\n19. Formula Flash\nTopic\nFormula / Recall\nMirror\n1/v + 1/u = 1/f\nLens\n1/v - 1/u = 1/f\nMirror magnification\nm = -v/u\nLens magnification\nm = v/u\nPower\nP = 1/f(m) D\nRefractive index\nμ = c/v\nCritical angle\nsin c = 1/μ\nPhoton energy\nE = hν\nPhoton momentum\np = h/λ\nFrequency-wavelength\nν = c/λ\n20. Final Exam Traps — 12 Red Flags\n• Mirror formula PLUS; lens formula MINUS\n• Rear-view = convex; shaving/dentist = concave\n• Convex mirror always virtual, erect, diminished\n• Concave mirror virtual image only when object lies between F and P\n• TIR requires denser\nrarer and incidence angle > critical\n→\n• Convex lens power positive; concave lens power negative\n• Violet most deviated/scattered; red least\n• Sky appears blue despite violet scattering more\n• Myopia concave; hypermetropia convex; presbyopia bifocal; astigmatism cylindrical\n• Retinal image = real + inverted\n• Polarisation proves light is transverse\n• Planck1900 photon vs Einstein1905 photoelectric; Nobel1921 photoelectric\nEXAM TRAP - “+/\n - Mirrors - TIR - Power - V/R - Sky - Eye Defects - Retina - Polarisation - Planck/Einstein”\n−\nChapter ke 12 highest-yield traps isi chain mein revise karo.\nMASTER MEMORY CHAIN — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\nMASTER - 1-Line Revision Chain\n“Light no-medium transverse EM + dual; c=3×10^8, visible3900-7800Å; Descartes/Newton particle, Huygens wave\n Reflection\n→\ni=r+same plane; plane mirror V-E-S-L\n concave/convex: rear-view convex, shaving/dentist concave; convex always V-E-D; concave\n→\nvirtual only F-P\n Mirror PLUS 1/v+1/u=1/f, f=R/2, m=-v/u\n Refraction denser=slower+toward normal, frequency same; Snell sin\n→\n→\ni/sin r=μ=c/v\n TIR denser\nrarer + i>c, sin c=1/μ; fibre/endoscope/diamond/mirage\n Convex lens + converge; concave - diverge;\n→\n→\n→\nLens MINUS 1/v-1/u=1/f, P=1/f D\n Dispersion VIBGYOR: violet most, red least; rainbow refraction+TIR+refraction; RGB light\nwhite;\n→\n→\nR-C/G-M/B-Y complementary\n Rayleigh shorter scatters more; sky blue, sunset/danger red\n Eye retina real-inverted, fovea sharp,\n→\n→\nblind spot zero, near point25cm; defects M-H-P-A = concave-convex-bifocal-cylindrical\n Microscope M=1+D/F; telescope objective\n→\nFo large, eyepiece Fe small\n Young1801 interference; diffraction edge-bending; polarisation proves transverse\n Planck1900\n→\n→\nphoton E=hν, p=h/λ; Einstein1905 photoelectric, Nobel1921 for photoelectric.”\nTOP 5: Mirror PLUS vs Lens MINUS | Rear-view convex / Shaving concave | TIR D\nR + i>c | M-H-P-A corrections | Planck1900 vs\n→\nEinstein1905/Nobel1921.","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.12 Semiconductor\nSEMICONDUCTOR — MEMORY TRICK SHEET\nGeneral Science · Physics · Chapter 12 · Intrinsic + Extrinsic + Doping + n-Type/p-Type + Photodiode + Zener + LED + Solar Cell\nYe full notes nahi hai — source chapter ke intrinsic/extrinsic semiconductors, doping, n-Type/p-Type carrier traps, temperature\nbehaviour aur semiconductor devices/principles ko fast exam-recall tricks mein compress kiya gaya hai.\n1. Conductor - Semiconductor - Insulator\nMaterial\nConductivity\nBand Gap\nTemperature Effect\nExamples\nConductor\nHigh\nZero / overlapping\nResistance increases\nAg, Cu, Au, Al\nSemiconductor\nMedium\nSmall (~1 eV)\nResistance decreases\nSi, Ge\nInsulator\nVery low\nLarge (>5 eV)\nMinimal change\nGlass, Rubber, Diamond\nTRICK - “C-S-I = High-Medium-Low Conductivity”\nConductor > Semiconductor > Insulator.\nTRICK - “Semiconductor Heats\n Resistance Drops”\n→\nTemperature\n\n semiconductor conductivity\n, resistance\n.\n↑→\n↑\n↓\nSource contrasts this with conductor, where conductivity decreases / resistance rises.\nEXAM TRAP -\nDiamond = insulator, semiconductor nahi.\n2. Intrinsic vs Extrinsic — Pure vs Doped\nFeature\nIntrinsic\nExtrinsic\nPurity\nPure Si or Ge\nDoped Si or Ge\nImpurity\nNone\nAdded intentionally\nCarrier balance\nElectrons = holes\nOne carrier becomes majority\nConductivity\nRelatively low\nHigher\nMain types\n—\nn-Type, p-Type\nTRICK - “Intrinsic = In-Pure; Extrinsic = Extra Impurity”\nIntrinsic = pure semiconductor.\nExtrinsic = doped semiconductor.\nTRICK - “Si-Ge = Pure Pair”\nSilicon + Germanium = intrinsic examples.\nEXAM TRAP - “0 K Intrinsic = Perfect Insulator”\nAt absolute zero free carriers absent.\n3. Doping — Small Impurity, Big Conductivity\n• Doping = pure Si/Ge mein very small impurity add karna\n• Purpose = available charge carriers increase karna\n conductivity increase\n→\n• Added impurity = dopant\n• Extrinsic semiconductor doping ke baad banta hai\nTRICK - “Doping = Dopant Added\n Carriers Added”\n→\nConductivity increase is the exam core.","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.12 Semiconductor\n4. n-Type — Penta Gives Extra Electron\nPoint\nn-Type\nDopant valency\nPentavalent = 5\nCommon dopants\nArsenic (As), Antimony (Sb), Phosphorus (P)\nBond idea\n4 electrons bond; 5th becomes extra free electron\nMajority carrier\nElectrons (-)\nMinority carrier\nHoles\nMeaning of n\nNegative carriers = electrons\nTRICK - “n = 5\n Extra Electron”\n→\nPentavalent dopant gives one extra electron.\nTRICK - “A-S-P = n-Type”\nArsenic - Antimony - Phosphorus.\nEXAM TRAP -\nPhosphorus doping gives n-Type.\n5. p-Type — Tri Leaves One Hole\nPoint\np-Type\nDopant valency\nTrivalent = 3\nCommon dopants\nAluminium (Al), Boron (B), Gallium (Ga), Indium (In)\nBond idea\nOne electron missing\n hole\n→\nMajority carrier\nHoles (+)\nMinority carrier\nElectrons\nMeaning of p\nPositive carriers = holes\nTRICK - “p = 3\n One Hole”\n→\nTrivalent dopant leaves one bond incomplete.\nTRICK - “A-B-G-I = p-Type”\nAluminium - Boron - Gallium - Indium.\nEXAM TRAP -\nAluminium gives p-Type even though aluminium itself is a good conductor.\n6. n-Type vs p-Type — 5E vs 3H\nProperty\nn-Type\np-Type\nDopant\nPentavalent (5)\nTrivalent (3)\nExamples\nAs, Sb, P\nAl, B, Ga, In\nMajority carrier\nElectrons\nHoles\nMinority carrier\nHoles\nElectrons\nCarrier sign\nNegative\nPositive\nOverall charge\nNeutral\nNeutral\nTRICK - “5\n Electron; 3\n Hole”\n→\n→\nn = pentavalent + electrons.\np = trivalent + holes.","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.12 Semiconductor\nEXAM TRAP - “Carrier Sign ≠ Net Charge”\nBoth n-Type and p-Type electrically neutral overall.\n7. Photodiode — Light Detection\nItem\nPhotodiode\nPurpose\nOptical / light detection\nConversion\nLight\n electrical signal\n→\nPrinciple\nPhotoelectric effect\nUse\nLight intensity detection; light-operated keys\nTRICK - “Photodiode = PHOTOelectric = Detect Light”\nPhotoelectric effect.\n8. Zener Diode — Reverse Bias Regulator\nItem\nZener Diode\nMain purpose\nVoltage regulation / voltage control\nOperation\nReverse bias\nRegion\nReverse-breakdown region\nResult\nNearly constant load voltage\nTRICK - “Zener = Zero-Worry Voltage in Reverse”\nReverse-bias breakdown\n voltage regulation.\n→\nEXAM TRAP -\nZener ko ordinary forward-biased diode ki tarah treat mat karna.\n9. LED — Electrical to Light\nItem\nLED\nFull form\nLight Emitting Diode\nConversion\nElectrical energy\n radiant/light energy\n→\nPrinciple\nElectroluminescence\nInventor\nNick Holonyak\nUses\nRemote control, burglar alarm, optical communication\nTRICK - “LED = Electricity\n Light = Electroluminescence”\n→\nInventor = Nick Holonyak.\nEXAM TRAP -\nLED principle = Electroluminescence; photoelectric/photovoltaic nahi.\n10. Solar Cell — Light to Electrical\nItem\nSolar Cell\nConversion\nSolar/light energy\n electrical energy\n→\nPrinciple\nPhotovoltaic effect\nUses\nRemote-area power generation; artificial-satellite batteries\nTRICK - “Solar = PhotoVOLTAIC = Light\n Voltage/Electricity”\n→\nPhotovoltaic effect.","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.12 Semiconductor\n11. Device Principle Master — P-Z-L-S\nDevice\nEnergy / Function\nPrinciple\nPhotodiode\nLight\n electrical signal\n→\nPhotoelectric effect\nZener diode\nVoltage regulation\nReverse breakdown\nLED\nElectrical\n light\n→\nElectroluminescence\nSolar cell\nLight\n electrical\n→\nPhotovoltaic effect\nTRICK - “PhotoDIODE = Photoelectric; Solar = Photovoltaic; LED = Electroluminescence”\nTeen similar-looking principles ko alag-alag lock karo.\nEXAM TRAP - “Zener = Reverse Breakdown”\nFunction-based fourth device.\n12. Temperature Behaviour — Semiconductor vs Conductor\nMaterial\nTemperature ↑\nResult\nSemiconductor\nConductivity ↑\nResistance ↓\nConductor\nConductivity ↓\nResistance ↑\nTRICK - “Semi Hot = Conduct More; Metal Hot = Resist More”\nOpposite temperature behaviour.\nEXAM TRAP -\nSemiconductor conductivity temperature ke saath increases.\n13. Rapid-Fire Recall\nQuestion\nAnswer\nKey intrinsic semiconductors?\nSilicon, Germanium\nDoping means?\nAdding impurity\nAdded impurity called?\nDopant\nn-Type valency?\nPentavalent 5\nn-Type majority?\nElectrons\np-Type valency?\nTrivalent 3\np-Type majority?\nHoles\nAluminium doping?\np-Type\nPhosphorus doping?\nn-Type\nIntrinsic at 0 K?\nPerfect insulator\nPhotodiode principle?\nPhotoelectric effect\nZener use?\nVoltage regulation\nLED principle?\nElectroluminescence\nLED inventor?\nNick Holonyak\nSolar-cell principle?\nPhotovoltaic effect","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.12 Semiconductor\n14. Final Exam Traps — 10 Red Flags\n• Semiconductor resistance decreases as temperature increases\n• Silicon and Germanium = intrinsic semiconductor examples\n• At 0 K intrinsic semiconductor = perfect insulator\n• n-Type = pentavalent 5; electrons majority\n• p-Type = trivalent 3; holes majority\n• Aluminium gives p-Type; Phosphorus gives n-Type\n• n-Type/p-Type overall electrically neutral\n• Photodiode = photoelectric effect\n• LED = electroluminescence; inventor Nick Holonyak\n• Solar cell = photovoltaic; Zener = reverse-bias voltage regulation\nEXAM TRAP - “Temp-SiGe-0K-5E-3H-Al/P-Neutral-PhotoD-LED-Solar/Zener”\nChapter ke 10 highest-yield traps isi order mein revise karo.\nMASTER MEMORY CHAIN — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\nMASTER - 1-Line Revision Chain\n“C-S-I = high-medium-low conductivity; semiconductor small ~1eV band gap, Si-Ge examples, heat\n conductivity\n resistance\n;\n↑\n↑\n↓\ndiamond insulator\n Intrinsic = pure Si/Ge, e=holes, low conductivity, 0K perfect insulator; Extrinsic = doped, higher conductivity,\n→\nn/p types\n Doping = tiny impurity add\n carriers\n\n conductivity\n\n n-Type: PENTA5, A-S-P = As-Sb-P, one extra electron,\n→\n→\n↑→\n↑→\nelectrons majority, holes minority\n p-Type: TRI3, A-B-G-I = Al-B-Ga-In, one hole, holes majority, electrons minority\n both overall\n→\n→\nneutral\n Photodiode light\nelectrical by photoelectric; Zener reverse-breakdown voltage regulator; LED electrical\nlight by\n→\n→\n→\nelectroluminescence, Nick Holonyak; Solar light\nelectrical by photovoltaic\n Master split: PhotoD=Photoelectric,\n→\n→\nSolar=Photovoltaic, LED=Electroluminescence, Zener=Reverse Breakdown.”\nTOP 5: Heat\n\n semiconductor R\n | n-Type 5/electron vs p-Type 3/hole | Both neutral | Photodiode-photoelectric vs Solar-photovoltaic vs\n↑→\n↓\nLED-electroluminescence | Zener reverse-bias regulator.","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.13 Our Universe\nOUR UNIVERSE — MEMORY TRICK SHEET\nGeneral Science · Physics · Chapter 13 · Big Bang + Galaxies + Stars + Sun + Solar System + India & Space\nYe full notes nahi hai — source chapter ke universe theories, red/blue shift, galaxy types, star facts, Sun, planets aur Indian space\nmissions ke high-memory names, years, numbers aur exam traps ko fast recall format mein compress kiya gaya hai.\n1. Universe — Number Hooks\nFact\nSource Answer\nAge of Universe\n13.8 billion years\nBig Bang\nGeorge Lemaitre — 1930\nHubble constant\n67 km/s/Mpc\nTotal stars\n 10^22\n≈\nOur galaxy\nMilky Way — Spiral\nNearest star after Sun\nProxima Centauri\nBrightest night-sky star\nSirius — Dog Star\nSunlight to Earth\n8 min 20 sec\nChandrasekhar limit\n1.44 solar masses\nTRICK - “13.8 - 67 - 10²² - 8:20 - 1.44”\nUniverse age\n Hubble constant\n stars\n sunlight time\n Chandrasekhar limit.\n→\n→\n→\n→\n2. Universe Theories — P-C-H-H-L\nTheory\nPropounder\nYear / Core\nGeocentric\nPtolemy\nAncient; Earth at centre\nHeliocentric\nCopernicus\n1543; Sun at centre\nHerschel Theory\nHerschel\n1785; Milky Way shape/structure\nHubble Theory\nEdwin Hubble\n1925; expanding universe\nBig Bang\nGeorge Lemaitre\n1930; origin ~13.8 bya\nTRICK - “P-C-H-H-L = Earth-Sun-Galaxy-Expansion-Origin”\nPtolemy Earth-centre; Copernicus Sun-centre; Herschel Milky Way; Hubble expansion; Lemaitre Big Bang.\nEXAM TRAP - “Big Bang = Lemaitre; Expansion = Hubble”\nYe do facts alag-alag hain.\n3. Red Shift - Blue Shift - Hubble\nConcept\nMeaning\nExam Interpretation\nRed Shift\nLonger/red wavelength\nMoving away\nBlue Shift\nShorter/blue wavelength\nComing closer\nHubble Law\nV = Hd\nRecession velocity = H × distance\nTRICK - “RED = Recedes; BLUE = Back Toward Us”\nMost distant galaxies red shift; source gives Andromeda as blue-shift example.\nEXAM TRAP - “V = H d; H = 67”\nSource Hubble constant = 67 km/s/Mpc.\n4. Galaxies — E-S-I\nType\nShape\nStars/Colour\nSource Example\nElliptical\nOval\nOld; yellow-red; minimum formation\nMaffei-1\nSpiral\nSpiral arms\nArms young/blue; centre old/red;\nmaximum formation\nMilky Way\nIrregular\nNo regular shape\nMixed; variable; intermediate\nformation\nSextans A","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.13 Our Universe\nTRICK - “E-S-I = Maffei - Milky - Sextans”\nElliptical Maffei-1; Spiral Milky Way; Irregular Sextans A.\nEXAM TRAP - “Spiral Arms Blue; Centre Red”\nYoung hot stars in arms; older stars in central bulge.\n5. Star Facts — P-S-Pole-Sun\nCue\nAnswer\nNearest star after Sun\nProxima Centauri\nBrightest night-sky star\nSirius — Dog Star\nPole Star\nNorthern direction; navigation\nSun\nYellow star of Milky Way\nEXAM TRAP - “Proxima = Nearest; Sirius = Brightest”\nNearest aur brightest ko interchange mat karna.\n6. Star Composition + Colour Temperature\nItem\nSource Recall\nHydrogen\n70%\nHelium\n28%\nCarbon\n1.5%\nNitrogen + Neon\n0.5%\nRed stars\nCoolest\n 3000-4000 K\n≈\nYellow stars\n 5000-6000 K; Sun yellow\n≈\nBlue stars\nHottest > 10,000 K\nTRICK - “H-He-C-NN = 70-28-1.5-0.5”\nSource star-gas composition.\nTRICK - “Red Cool - Yellow Medium - Blue Hot”\nColour-temperature order.\n7. Black Hole + Chandrasekhar\n• Black hole gravity itni strong ki light bhi escape nahi kar sakti\n• Chandrasekhar limit = 1.44 solar masses\n• Named after Subrahmanyan Chandrasekhar\n• At/below limit: white dwarf can remain stable\n• Sufficiently massive star beyond limit: after supernova collapse can produce neutron star or black hole\nTRICK - “Chandra = 1.44 Sun”\nWhite-dwarf stability limit.\nEXAM TRAP -\nChandrasekhar limit 1.44 solar masses; nearest star fact se mix mat karna.\n8. Constellations — U-O-C\n• Constellation = recognisable group/pattern of stars\n• Saptarishi = Ursa Major\n• Orion = source notes guide toward Sirius\n• Cassiopeia = important example","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.13 Our Universe\nTRICK - “U-O-C = Ursa Major - Orion - Cassiopeia”\nSaptarishi = Ursa Major.\n9. Sun — 109 / 28 / 8:20 / Photo\nSun Fact\nSource Value\nDiameter\n 109 × Earth diameter\n≈\nGravity\n 28 × Earth gravity\n≈\nLight travel time\n8 min 20 sec\nVisible surface\nPhotosphere\nEnergy source\nNuclear fusion: H\n He\n→\nSource composition\nH 71%, He 26.5%, others 2.5%\nTRICK - “Sun = 109 - 28 - 8:20 - PHOTO”\nDiameter, gravity, light time, visible surface.\nEXAM TRAP - “Photosphere Visible; Chromosphere + Corona Above”\nVisible surface = Photosphere.\n10. Planets Order — M-V-E-M-J-S-U-N\n• Mercury\n Venus\n Earth\n Mars\n Jupiter\n Saturn\n Uranus\n Neptune\n→\n→\n→\n→\n→\n→\n→\nTRICK - “M-V-E-M-J-S-U-N”\nSun se outward 8 major planets ka order.\nEXAM TRAP -\nPluto = dwarf planet; eight major planets mein nahi.\n11. Inner vs Outer — M-V-E-M / J-S-U-N\nFeature\nInner / Terrestrial\nOuter / Jovian\nMembers\nMercury, Venus, Earth, Mars\nJupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune\nNature\nRocky, dense\nMostly gaseous / giant\nSize\nGenerally smaller\nMuch larger\nMoons/rings\nFewer\nMany\nTRICK - “Inner = MVEM Rocky; Outer = JSUN Giant”\nFour-four planet split.\n12. Planet High-Yield Facts\nPlanet\nSource Memory Hook\nMercury\nClosest + smallest; source notes no atmosphere; huge temperature variation\nVenus\nHottest + brightest; greenhouse; retrograde rotation\nEarth\nLife + one moon + Blue Planet\nMars\nRed due iron oxide; Phobos + Deimos; MOM\nJupiter\nLargest; Great Red Spot; source lists 95 moons\nSaturn\nLeast dense major planet; rings; source lists 146 moons\nUranus\nRotates nearly on side; tilt ~98°; very cold\nNeptune\nFarthest major planet; strongest winds\nTRICK - “Venus HOT; Jupiter BIG; Mercury SMALL”\nHottest ≠ closest.\nTRICK - “Mars Red; Earth Blue; Uranus Sideways; Neptune Winds”\nColour + motion + weather hooks.","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.13 Our Universe\nEXAM TRAP -\nHottest planet = Venus, not Mercury.\n13. India & Space — Mission Chain\nMission / Satellite\nSource Detail\nSignificance\nMOM / Mangalyaan\nMars Orbiter Mission; 2013\nIndia first interplanetary mission\nGISAT-1\nGeo Imaging Satellite-1\nSource lists as India first satellite in geostationary\norbit\nAPPLE\nAriane Passenger Payload Experiment\nIndia first geostationary telecommunication\nsatellite\nChandrayaan-1\nMoon mission; 2008\nEvidence of water molecules on Moon\nChandrayaan-3\nMoon lander; 2023\nSoft landing in lunar south-polar region; source\nnotes first country near south pole\nAditya-L1\nSolar mission; 2023\nIndia first dedicated solar observatory mission\nTRICK - “MOM = Mangalyaan = Mars = 2013”\nIndia first interplanetary mission.\nTRICK - “APPLE = Ariane Passenger Payload Experiment”\nFull form ko exact yaad karo.\nTRICK - “C1 Water; C3 South Pole; A-L1 Sun”\nChandrayaan-1\n water evidence; Chandrayaan-3\n south-polar landing; Aditya-L1\n solar observatory.\n→\n→\n→\n14. APPLE vs GISAT-1\nSatellite\nSource Distinction\nAPPLE\nFirst geostationary telecommunication satellite named in source\nGISAT-1\nListed separately as Geo Imaging Satellite-1 and India first satellite in\ngeostationary orbit\nEXAM TRAP - “APPLE = Telecom; GISAT = Geo Imaging”\nDono ko same label mat karna.\n15. Rapid-Fire Revision\nQuestion / Cue\nAnswer\nBig Bang\nGeorge Lemaitre — 1930\nHeliocentric\nCopernicus — 1543\nGeocentric\nPtolemy\nHubble Law\nV = Hd\nAge Universe\n13.8 billion years\nOur galaxy\nMilky Way — Spiral\nNearest star after Sun\nProxima Centauri\nBrightest night star\nSirius\nChandrasekhar limit\n1.44 solar masses\nSun visible surface\nPhotosphere\nSunlight to Earth\n8 min 20 sec\nHottest / largest / smallest\nVenus / Jupiter / Mercury\nRed / Blue planet\nMars / Earth\nIndia first interplanetary\nMangalyaan / MOM\nAPPLE full form\nAriane Passenger Payload Experiment","ExamFusion Prep · Physics Memory Tricks · Ch.13 Our Universe\n16. Final Exam Traps — 12 Red Flags\n• Big Bang = George Lemaitre; Hubble = expansion\n• Red shift = away; Blue shift = approaching\n• Milky Way = Spiral galaxy\n• Nearest star after Sun = Proxima Centauri; brightest night star = Sirius\n• Red stars coolest; Blue stars hottest\n• Chandrasekhar limit = 1.44 solar masses\n• Sun visible surface = Photosphere\n• Sunlight reaches Earth in 8 min 20 sec\n• Hottest = Venus; largest = Jupiter; smallest = Mercury\n• Pluto = dwarf planet\n• MOM = Mangalyaan = India first interplanetary mission\n• APPLE = Ariane Passenger Payload Experiment; do not confuse with GISAT-1\nEXAM TRAP - “Lemaitre/Hubble - Red/Blue - Milky - Proxima/Sirius - Star Colour - 1.44 - Photo - 8:20 - V/J/M -\nPluto - MOM - APPLE”\nChapter ke 12 highest-yield traps isi sequence mein revise karo.\nMASTER MEMORY CHAIN — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\nMASTER - 1-Line Revision Chain\n“Universe 13.8 bya; Big Bang Lemaitre1930, Hubble expansion1925 + V=Hd, H=67; theories P-C-H-H-L = Ptolemy-Copernicus-\nHerschel-Hubble-Lemaitre\n Red away, Blue toward; Andromeda blue-shift example\n Galaxies E-S-I = Maffei-1 / Milky Way /\n→\n→\nSextans A; Milky spiral arms blue, centre red\n Stars: Proxima nearest after Sun, Sirius brightest night/Dog Star, Pole Star north;\n→\ngases H-He-C-NN = 70-28-1.5-0.5; Red cool, Yellow medium, Blue hot\n Black hole light cannot escape; Chandra limit1.44Sun;\n→\nconstellations Ursa Major/Saptarishi-Orion-Cassiopeia\n Sun 109×diameter, 28×gravity, light8:20, Photosphere visible, fusion H\nHe;\n→\n→\nsource comp71/26.5/2.5\n Planets M-V-E-M-J-S-U-N; inner MVEM rocky vs outer JSUN giant; Venus hottest, Jupiter largest, Mercury\n→\nsmallest; Mars red, Earth blue, Uranus side98°, Neptune strongest winds; Pluto dwarf\n India space: MOM=Mangalyaan2013 first\n→\ninterplanetary; C1-2008 water evidence; C3-2023 south-polar soft landing; Aditya-L1-2023 solar; APPLE=Ariane Passenger Payload\nExperiment telecom vs GISAT-1 Geo Imaging.”\nTOP 5: Lemaitre Big Bang vs Hubble expansion | Proxima nearest vs Sirius brightest | Sun Photosphere + 8:20 | Venus hot/Jupiter\nbig/Mercury small | MOM + APPLE full form."];
